{"_id":"9a9b1151914ebc1d","name":"Tamer's Bond Core Rulebook","pages":[{"_id":"rb001theworld0001","name":"Chapter 1: The World","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 1: The World</h1>\n<p>Before you create your Tamer or choose a Digimon partner, it helps to understand the world you're stepping into.</p>\n<p><em>Tamer's Bond</em> takes place across two worlds: the Human World and the Digital World. They're connected, but exactly how that connection works is up to the campaign you're playing.</p>\n<h2>The Digital World</h2>\n<p>The Digital World exists alongside the Human World, formed from the enormous amount of data that flows through modern civilization. It's a place where the rules people are used to don't always apply.</p>\n<p>The landscape can be beautiful, dangerous, or just plain weird. Mountains made from crystalized data can stretch across the horizon, oceans of code can change shape, and entire areas can look completely different from one day to the next.</p>\n<p>The Digital World isn't meant to feel like a normal fantasy world with a different coat of paint. It's a living, changing place, and its inhabitants can have a real effect on the environment around them.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The Digital World Is Not Fixed</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>The Digital World can be changed by those who understand how it works.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>A powerful Digimon might tear apart a mountain range and reshape it into a single massive peak. A Tamer who has learned enough about the world's underlying code might find a way to open a passage between two distant locations.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>To someone from the Human World, this might look like magic. In the Digital World, it's simply part of how things work.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>This also means that the Digital World your group visits at the beginning of a campaign doesn't have to look the same by the end of it. The characters can leave their mark on the world, for better or worse.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>The GM decides what is possible within the campaign and how much the Digital World can be changed. The players decide what their characters do with that freedom.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>The Human World</h2>\n<p>The Human World is whatever you decide it is for your campaign. Your story might take place in a major city, a small town, or even a single neighborhood where strange things have started happening.</p>\n<p>Unlike the Digital World, the Human World is generally stable. Buildings don't normally move overnight, streets stay where they were built, and reality tends to behave the way people expect it to.</p>\n<p>How Digimon fit into human society is one of the first decisions the GM should make.</p>\n<p>Maybe Digimon are already a normal part of everyday life. Maybe only a handful of people know they exist. Or maybe most people think Digimon are nothing more than rumors and strange stories on the internet.</p>\n<p>There isn't one correct answer. Pick whatever works best for the kind of campaign you want to run.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Setting Question</strong></th><th><strong>What It Changes</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Do humans know Digimon exist?</td><td>This affects how Tamers and their partners are treated. A Digimon walking into a convenience store is very different when everyone already knows what Digimon are.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can people travel between the two worlds freely?</td><td>This determines how often the campaign moves between the Human World and the Digital World.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Do governments and other authorities know about Tamers?</td><td>Tamers might be ignored, watched, recruited, or even treated as a threat.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>How common are Digimon in everyday life?</td><td>This helps establish the tone of the campaign, from a world full of wonder and discovery to one where humans and Digimon simply live alongside each other.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Digimon</h2>\n<p>Digimon are digital lifeforms born from data. They can think, feel, form relationships, learn from their experiences, and change as they grow.</p>\n<p>Most Digimon have some desire to become stronger, although what \"stronger\" means to them can be very different from one Digimon to another. Some want to become powerful fighters. Others want to protect their friends, reach a particular evolution, or simply find a place where they can live peacefully.</p>\n<p>Evolution is one of the most important parts of a Digimon's life. A Digimon can grow into stronger forms as it gains experience and reaches the conditions necessary to evolve. When a Digimon is defeated, it may eventually return to a Digi-Egg and begin its journey again.</p>\n<p>That cycle is a normal part of life in the Digital World.</p>\n<p>What makes each Digimon interesting, however, is what they want beyond simply becoming stronger.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>What a Digimon Wants</strong></th><th><strong>What That Looks Like in Play</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>To become the strongest</td><td>The Digimon looks for powerful opponents and may challenge other Digimon simply because it wants to test itself. A challenge doesn't necessarily mean the Digimon is hostile.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>To protect something or someone</td><td>The Digimon may be protective of a particular person, place, or group. It might be suspicious of strangers and become aggressive when what it protects is threatened.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>To evolve into a specific form</td><td>The Digimon has a personal goal tied to its evolution. This could influence where it travels, who it works with, and even which risks it is willing to take.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>To live peacefully</td><td>The Digimon has little interest in fighting. That doesn't mean it is helpless, though. When something it cares about is threatened, it may show a completely different side of itself.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>To understand humans</td><td>The Digimon is curious about the Human World and the people who live there. This curiosity can eventually develop into a strong bond with its Tamer.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Something entirely its own</td><td>Not every Digimon needs to fit neatly into one of these examples. Work with your GM to create something that fits your character and the campaign.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":100,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb002overview0002","name":"Chapter 2: Overview","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 2: Overview</h1>\n<p>In <em>Tamer's Bond</em>, each player controls two characters: a Tamer and their Digimon partner.</p>\n<p>Both characters can take part in combat, although Digimon are usually the ones doing most of the fighting. That doesn't mean Tamers are helpless. What your Tamer does, how you spend Hope, and how you develop your partner can all have a big impact on the outcome of a battle.</p>\n<p>The two characters are meant to work together. Your Tamer's abilities directly affect your Digimon, so building one is also part of building the other.</p>\n<h2>What You Need to Play</h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Players</strong></th><th><strong>One GM and 1–4 players</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Dice</td><td>d4, d6, d8, d10, d12, d20, d100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Character Sheets</td><td>One for each Tamer and Digimon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grid Map</td><td>Used for combat. 1 square = 1 space.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tokens</td><td>Miniatures or tokens for all participants</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>The Core Relationship</h2>\n<p>Every Tamer has six Crest Stats. These stats are more than just numbers on the Tamer's sheet, they directly affect their Digimon partner.</p>\n<p>When your Tamer grows stronger, their Digimon grows with them.</p>\n<p>Each Crest Stat is tied to a specific part of your Digimon's abilities. Some affect combat directly, while others determine things like movement, health, or how difficult the Digimon is to hit.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Tamer Crest Stat</strong></th><th><strong>Feeds Into Digimon</strong></th><th><strong>What It Does in Combat</strong></th><th><strong>Notes</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Courage</td><td>Courage (Hit Rate)</td><td>Adds to attack rolls when determining whether an attack hits. Roll 1d20 + Courage against the target's Reliability + 10.</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Friendship</td><td>Friendship (Speed)</td><td>Determines movement and turn order.</td><td>Digimon move a number of spaces equal to their Friendship. Tamers move Friendship × 2 spaces. Digimon turn order = Friendship. Tamer turn order = Friendship × 2.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Love</td><td>Love (Damage Reduction)</td><td>Reduces the damage taken from incoming attacks.</td><td>Subtract Love from incoming damage on every hit.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>Knowledge (Damage)</td><td>Adds to the damage dealt by attacks.</td><td>Knowledge is the primary damage bonus for attacks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sincerity</td><td>Sincerity (HP)</td><td>Determines the Digimon's total Hit Points.</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Reliability (Miss Threshold)</td><td>Determines how difficult the Digimon is to hit.</td><td>An attacker must meet or beat Reliability + 10 to successfully hit.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hope (derived)</td><td>Digivolution Resource</td><td>Provides the resource used to digivolve.</td><td>Hope is not a combat stat. See the Hope Pool table for more information.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":200,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb003whoisatamer03","name":"Chapter 3: Who Is a Tamer?","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 3: Who Is a Tamer?</h1>\n<p>A Tamer is someone who has formed a real bond with a Digimon. Not ownership, and not partnership in the loose sense of two people who just happen to work together. What a Tamer believes, wants, and is willing to risk shapes what their partner becomes, both in the fiction and at the table. Before you touch a single number, this chapter is about putting an actual person on the page.</p>\n<p>Every player builds one of these. By the end of Chapter 6 you'll have a complete Tamer, and Chapter 7 is where you build the Digimon standing next to them.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Starting EXP Budget</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Every new Tamer begins with 1,500 EXP to spend during creation. All six Crest Stats and all 24 skills start at rank 1 for free; that EXP is what takes them beyond the baseline, and you'll spend it across this chapter and the two after it.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Your Digimon partner gets the same deal. They start with their own 1,500 EXP, spent separately in Chapter 7. Nothing you spend on yourself comes out of their pool, and nothing they spend comes out of yours.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Who a Tamer Interacts With</h2>\n<p><strong>Their Digimon.</strong> This is the relationship the whole game is built around. Your Crest Stats feed directly into your partner's combat stats, your Hope keeps them digivolved, and your choices about how hard to push them matter as much as anything they roll. Chapter 5 covers exactly how that link works, stat by stat.</p>\n<p><strong>Other Tamers.</strong> Most groups run with one to four players, each with their own Tamer and partner. You're not just fighting alongside each other. A Tamer's skills and class abilities regularly exist to support the party as a whole, not just their own Digimon.</p>\n<p><strong>Everyone else.</strong> NPCs, rival Tamers, wild Digimon, the world the GM is running. A Tamer's skills are what let them navigate all of that without a Digimon doing the talking: reading a lie, calming someone down, following a trail. That's the ground Chapter 6 covers.</p>\n<h2>Building Your Tamer</h2>\n<p>Start with who they are before you touch a single number. The Digital World tends to respond to conviction as much as it responds to raw strength, and a Tamer's want, their fear, and the flaw that's actually going to cause problems all shape the story as much as anything on the stat line.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Name</td><td>What do people call you?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Age</td><td>Most Tamers are 10 to 14. No hard rule.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gender & Pronouns</td><td>How do you identify?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appearance</td><td>What do people notice first?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personality</td><td>Two or three words that sum you up.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Background</td><td>Where are you from? What was life like before?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Want</td><td>What does your character want most deeply at their core?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fear</td><td>What do they fear most? Be specific.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flaw</td><td>One honest weakness that will actually cause problems.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Crest</td><td>Which of the six Crest Stats defines you?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Take the Crest question seriously. It's more than flavor: your Crest is almost always the stat you'll raise first and fastest, which means it's also the stat most likely to set the ceiling for how far you can push your favorite skills (Chapter 6 covers exactly how). Picking a Crest means picking the trait your Tamer leans on when things get hard, and every class tree in every season book is built around one.</p>\n<p>Once you know who your Tamer is, the next step is picking what they can actually do beyond raw stats and skills. That's Chapter 4: Classes, and once that's settled, Chapter 5: Crest Stats & Hope is where the actual numbers start.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":300,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb004classes00004","name":"Chapter 4: Classes","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 4: Classes</h1>\n<p>Classes are the part of a Tamer's character that represents <strong>how they grew</strong>.</p>\n<p>Crest Stats describe a Tamer's natural strengths. Skills describe what they know how to do. Classes are different: a class represents the habits, lessons, relationships, and turning points that shaped a Tamer over the course of their adventure.</p>\n<p>Each season book contains its own classes, based on the Tamers and character arcs from that season. A class might be built around Courage, Friendship, Love, or another defining trait, but the rules for using classes are the same in every season.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>What Is a Class?</h2>\n<p>A class is a progression of abilities arranged into a small tree.</p>\n<p>Every class contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A Core Class</strong>, which establishes the class's theme and gives its first ability.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier 2</strong>, which expands on the Core Class.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier 3</strong>, which represents the class's strongest and most specialized abilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You do not need to complete the entire tree. A Tamer can stop after taking the Core Class, continue into one tier, or eventually invest heavily enough to reach Tier 3.</p>\n<p>The complete class trees are found in the season books.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Class Requirements</h2>\n<p>Every Core Class and class ability lists its requirements.</p>\n<p>A requirement might include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A minimum Crest Stat rank.</li>\n<li>A specific class ability.</li>\n<li>A combination of both.</li>\n<li>A special game rule, such as having unlocked a particular Digivolution Stage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You must meet <strong>all</strong> listed requirements before purchasing an ability.</p>\n<p>If an ability does not list a requirement, there is no additional requirement beyond the normal class-tree rules.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Taking a Core Class</h2>\n<p>A Core Class is the foundation of a class.</p>\n<p>When you purchase a Core Class, you gain its ability immediately and become a member of that class.</p>\n<p>Core Classes normally require a Crest Stat rank and cost between <strong>200 and 300 EXP</strong>, although a special Core Class may use a different requirement when its theme calls for it.</p>\n<p>A Tamer may take the Core Class of any class they qualify for. You do not need to take classes in any particular order, and taking one class never prevents you from taking another.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Moving Up the Class Tree</h2>\n<p>You cannot skip tiers.</p>\n<p>To purchase an ability from <strong>Tier 2</strong>, you must already have the Core Class.</p>\n<p>To purchase an ability from <strong>Tier 3</strong>, you must already have at least one ability from Tier 2.</p>\n<p>You do <strong>not</strong> need to purchase every ability in a tier.</p>\n<p>For example, a Tamer could take:</p>\n<blockquote><p>Core Class → Tier 2 ability → stop</p></blockquote>\n<p>They could also take:</p>\n<blockquote><p>Core Class → Tier 2 ability → Tier 2 ability → Tier 3 ability</p></blockquote>\n<p>The class tree is a path of access, not a checklist.</p>\n<p>Some individual abilities may list additional prerequisites. When they do, those prerequisites must also be satisfied.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Spending EXP on Classes</h2>\n<p>Classes use the same EXP pool as everything else in character advancement.</p>\n<p>There is no separate Class EXP.</p>\n<p>Whenever you earn EXP, you can spend it on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increasing Crest Stats.</li>\n<li>Increasing Skills.</li>\n<li>Purchasing class abilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This means every class purchase is a real choice. Spending EXP on a class means having less EXP available for your other forms of advancement.</p>\n<p>The EXP Cost listed beside an ability is the amount you must spend to purchase it.</p>\n<p>Once purchased, a class ability is permanently part of your Tamer unless a rule specifically says otherwise.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Having Multiple Classes</h2>\n<p>A Tamer can have <strong>any number of classes</strong>.</p>\n<p>You do not have to finish one class before taking another. You can purchase a Core Class from one tree, return to it later, and eventually begin a completely different class.</p>\n<p>This is intentional. Classes are meant to describe the individual Tamer, not force every character into a single predetermined build.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Class Abilities and Actions</h2>\n<p>Class abilities use the terminology and action rules found elsewhere in this book.</p>\n<p>When an ability says <strong>Basic Action</strong>, <strong>Free Action</strong>, <strong>Reaction</strong>, <strong>attack roll</strong>, <strong>skill check</strong>, or another defined game term, use the normal rules for that term.</p>\n<p>If a class ability changes an existing rule, the class ability takes precedence for that specific situation.</p>\n<p>Unless an ability specifically says otherwise, its effects apply only to the Tamer who purchased it and their partner Digimon.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Building a Tamer Through Classes</h2>\n<p>There is no single correct way to build a Tamer.</p>\n<p>A class should reward a player for leaning into the kind of person their Tamer is becoming. The strongest choices are not necessarily the ones with the biggest numbers; they are the choices that make the Tamer feel more like the character they are trying to portray.</p>\n<p>A class might make a Tamer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Better at supporting their partner.</li>\n<li>More dangerous when their partner is pushed to the limit.</li>\n<li>Better at protecting other characters.</li>\n<li>More capable of solving problems outside combat.</li>\n<li>More effective at using Hope.</li>\n<li>Better at interacting with Digimon.</li>\n<li>Capable of actions that other Tamers simply cannot perform.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The exact abilities are different from season to season.</p>\n<p>The framework stays the same.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Season Books</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>The class trees themselves are presented in the season books. This chapter provides the universal rules for reading and advancing those trees.</p></blockquote>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":400,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb005creststatshp5","name":"Chapter 5: Crest Stats & Hope","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 5: Crest Stats & Hope</h1>\n<p>Every Tamer has six Crest Stats. They start at rank 1 for free and can climb as high as rank 10 over the life of a campaign, though nobody gets there in a single session. Each rank costs more than the last, so raising a stat is a real commitment, not something you top off casually between sessions:</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Upgrade</th><th>EXP Cost</th><th>Running Total</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 1 → 2</td><td>200</td><td>200</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 2 → 3</td><td>300</td><td>500</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 3 → 4</td><td>400</td><td>900</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 4 → 5</td><td>500</td><td>1,400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 5 → 6</td><td>600</td><td>2,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 6 → 7</td><td>700</td><td>2,700</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 7 → 8</td><td>800</td><td>3,500</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 8 → 9</td><td>900</td><td>4,400</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 9 → 10</td><td>1,000</td><td>5,400</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A starting Tamer with 1,500 EXP can only push one or two stats past rank 3 or 4. That's intentional. Check Chapter 9's pacing guide if you want a sense of how fast a stat should realistically climb over a full campaign.</p>\n<p>Crest Stats aren't just yours, either. Your Digimon partner uses the same six stats, and their total in each one is built partly from your rank in it (the \"Four Layer System\" in Chapter 8). Raise Courage on your own sheet, and your partner hits harder in the same fight. That's the core relationship this whole game runs on, so before you spend a single point, it helps to know exactly what each stat is doing on both sides of the table.</p>\n<h2>The Six Crest Stats</h2>\n<h3>Courage (CRG)</h3>\n<p>Courage is what it looks like when someone acts under pressure instead of freezing. In combat, it governs Hit Rate: both you and your Digimon roll 1d20 + Courage against a target's Reliability + 10 to land an attack (full sequence in Chapter 10). It's also the stat that determines how hard a [PUSH]-tagged move shoves someone around.</p>\n<p>Courage governs five skills: Blitz, Ironclad, Crusher, Ghost, and Roar.</p>\n<h3>Friendship (FRD)</h3>\n<p>Friendship measures how well someone reads others and moves with them. Mechanically, it governs Speed, setting both movement and turn order for you and your Digimon alike. Digimon move a number of spaces equal to their Friendship and act in initiative equal to that same number. Tamers move and act at double that rate, Friendship × 2 spaces and Friendship × 2 initiative. A Tamer with Friendship rank 3 moves 6 spaces and acts on initiative 6; their Digimon moves 3 spaces and acts on initiative 3. Ties in turn order are broken by whoever has the higher raw Friendship stat.</p>\n<p>Friendship governs three skills: Scan, Rally, and Broadcast.</p>\n<h3>Love (LVE)</h3>\n<p>Love is awareness and protectiveness, noticing what's about to happen to someone before it happens. In combat, it governs Damage Reduction: subtract your total Love from every hit of incoming damage. It also determines how long it takes to shake off a Freeze status effect.</p>\n<p>Love governs three skills: Mend, Radar, and Tame.</p>\n<h3>Knowledge (KNW)</h3>\n<p>Knowledge is tactics, technical understanding, and reading how the Digital World's underlying logic actually works. It's the primary source of Damage, added directly to the PR dice roll of every attack you or your Digimon land. It also governs how well a Confuse status effect can be resisted.</p>\n<p>Knowledge governs more skills than any other stat, seven in total: Decode, Jack In, Modify, Trace, Archive, Command, and Playback. That breadth is deliberate. Knowledge Tamers tend to be the ones holding the party's information together outside of combat as much as inside it.</p>\n<h3>Sincerity (SNC)</h3>\n<p>Sincerity is a character's core resistance, what they're actually made of underneath everything else. It's the stat behind HP for both halves of the pair: your Digimon's Max HP is built from total Sincerity (Chapter 8), and your own HP as a Tamer is derived from your Sincerity rank directly (see below). It also determines how well a Sleep status effect can be resisted, through the Firewall skill.</p>\n<p>Sincerity governs three skills: Firewall, Reinforce, and Core Drive.</p>\n<h3>Reliability (RLB)</h3>\n<p>Reliability is preparation and protection, the stat behind being hard to catch off guard. In combat, it sets the Miss Threshold: an attacker has to roll 1d20 + their Courage and meet or beat your Reliability + 10, or the attack simply doesn't land. It's the mirror image of Courage in the hit formula, and it's also the backbone of a lot of the party's actual healing and field survival.</p>\n<p>Reliability governs three skills: Zero Error, Field Ops, and Recovery.</p>\n<h2>Hope</h2>\n<p>Hope isn't one of the six Crest Stats, and it isn't tied to any of them either. It tracks your Tamer's overall growth: specifically, the total amount of EXP they've earned over their whole career, starting with their initial 1,500.</p>\n<p>It doesn't matter where that EXP ends up. Whether you pour everything into one Crest Stat, spread it across Skills, or sink it deep into a Class tree, every point you've ever earned counts toward your Hope Pool the same way.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Hope Doesn't Cost You Anything to Earn</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Your Hope Pool grows automatically as your total EXP earned goes up. You never spend EXP on Hope directly, and holding EXP in reserve instead of spending it doesn't shrink your Hope either. It's about what you've earned over your career, not what's currently sitting unspent.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Use the table below to find your Hope Pool. If your total EXP falls between two entries, use the value from the most recent milestone you've reached, not an average between them.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Total EXP Earned</th><th>Hope Pool</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1,500</td><td>20</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2,000</td><td>25</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3,000</td><td>35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4,000</td><td>40</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5,000</td><td>45</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7,500</td><td>55</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10,000</td><td>65</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15,000</td><td>80</td></tr>\n<tr><td>20,000</td><td>100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>30,000</td><td>125</td></tr>\n<tr><td>40,000</td><td>145</td></tr>\n<tr><td>50,000</td><td>165</td></tr>\n<tr><td>60,000</td><td>185</td></tr>\n<tr><td>70,000 and up</td><td>200</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>For example, a Tamer with 3,150 total EXP has passed the 3,000 milestone, so their Hope Pool is 35, even though they haven't reached 4,000 yet.</p>\n<p>Hope is what keeps your Digimon digivolved. It's spent automatically at the start of your turn in combat to cover the Hope cost of their current stage. If it ever hits 0, your partner immediately reverts to their Default Stage before they can act (full breakdown in Chapter 12: Digivolution, including what happens if you choose to push past what you can actually afford). It refills completely between sessions and after a long rest, so nobody starts a new session running on empty.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Version Note.</strong> Hope used to scale off your single highest Crest Stat rank, which meant a Tamer who spent EXP on Skills or a Class instead of stacking one stat fell behind on Hope for no real reason. Tying Hope to total EXP earned instead means every path through character growth grows your Hope at the same rate, whether that path is stat-heavy, skill-heavy, class-heavy, or a mix of all three.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Maximum Hope</h3>\n<p>A Tamer reaches maximum Hope at 70,000 total EXP earned, giving them a Hope Pool of 200. Reaching that milestone doesn't mean every Crest Stat, Skill, or Class is maxed out too, just that Hope itself has topped out.</p>\n<h2>Tamer HP</h2>\n<p>Your own HP is derived from your Sincerity rank: <strong>HP = 12 + (Sincerity rank × 4)</strong>. A rank 1 Tamer has 16 HP; a rank 10 Tamer has 52.</p>\n<h2>Temporary HP</h2>\n<p>Some effects grant <strong>Temporary HP</strong>: a separate pool layered on top of your normal HP. This applies to both Tamers and Digimon.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No cap.</strong> Temporary HP has no maximum. If something grants you more while you still have some, it stacks on top of whatever you currently have.</li>\n<li><strong>Absorbs damage first.</strong> Any damage you take is subtracted from Temporary HP before it touches your actual HP. Once Temporary HP hits 0, further damage starts coming out of your real HP as normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Resets on rest.</strong> Temporary HP drops to 0 when you rest, whether or not it was used up. It doesn't carry over.</li>\n</ul>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":500,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb006skills000006","name":"Chapter 6: Skills","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 6: Skills</h1>\n<p>A skill is a trained, specific application of a Crest Stat: the difference between being Courageous in general and being specifically good at vaulting a fence without breaking stride. Where a Crest Stat governs the big combat math (hit rate, damage, HP, and so on), skills are what let a Tamer act on everything else: reading a room, hacking a terminal, patching up an ally, standing their ground when everything is going wrong.</p>\n<p>Every skill starts at rank 1 for free, same as your stats. There are 24 of them in total, each tied to one of the six Crest Stats, and each one runs from rank 1 up to a cap of rank 6.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The Stat Cap Rule</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Through rank 5, a skill's rank can never exceed its parent Crest Stat's rank. If your Friendship is rank 3, none of your Friendship skills can go above rank 3 either. You have to raise the stat before you can raise the skills underneath it.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Rank 6, the top of the scale, is a special case. Pushing a skill all the way to rank 6 requires its parent Crest Stat to be at rank 9, not just rank 6. True mastery of a skill asks a lot more of the underlying stat than the normal step-by-step climb does.</p></blockquote>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Skill</th><th>Stat</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Blitz</td><td>Courage</td><td>Explosive speed and agility, vaulting, sprinting</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ironclad</td><td>Courage</td><td>Pushing through exhaustion and harsh conditions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Crusher</td><td>Courage</td><td>Raw force, breaking barriers, overpowering obstacles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ghost</td><td>Courage</td><td>Moving unseen, vanishing into shadows, tailing targets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Roar</td><td>Courage</td><td>Projecting dominance, making others hesitate or break</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scan</td><td>Friendship</td><td>Reading people like data, sensing deception</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rally</td><td>Friendship</td><td>Getting someone on your side through reason or charm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Broadcast</td><td>Friendship</td><td>Commanding attention, moving an audience</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mend</td><td>Love</td><td>Calming someone in distress, offering care that reaches them</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Radar</td><td>Love</td><td>Noticing fine details, reading a room, sensing danger early</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tame</td><td>Love</td><td>Connecting with Digimon or wild creatures without words</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decode</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>Breaking down problems, finding the flaw in any system</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jack In</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>Digital intrusion, breaching firewalls, hijacking networks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Modify</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>Building, repairing, improvising devices from parts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trace</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>Following trails, piecing together evidence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Archive</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>Deep knowledge of the Digital World, Digimon history and lore</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Command</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>Reading a battle, calling plays, coordinating allies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Playback</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>Pulling precise facts from memory</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Firewall</td><td>Sincerity</td><td>Blocking mental attacks, dark auras, psychological pressure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reinforce</td><td>Sincerity</td><td>Enduring physical damage and punishment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Core Drive</td><td>Sincerity</td><td>Refusing to give up when everything is falling apart</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zero Error</td><td>Reliability</td><td>Staying precise and controlled under pressure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Field Ops</td><td>Reliability</td><td>Surviving and navigating the Digital World's hostile terrain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Reliability</td><td>Treating injuries, getting downed allies back on their feet</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Making a Skill Check</h2>\n<p>When a skill applies to what your Tamer is trying to do, here's the roll: grab a number of d6 equal to your rank in that skill, roll them all, and add them together. A rank 3 skill rolls 3d6. A rank 6 skill, the maximum, rolls 6d6.</p>\n<p>The GM looks at what you're attempting and sets a Difficulty Number, or DN, based on how hard the fiction says the task actually is. Add up your dice and compare the total to the DN. Meet or beat it and you succeed. Fall short and you don't.</p>\n<p>Because your result comes from adding up a pile of dice instead of one die plus a flat bonus, a single point of skill rank matters more here than it would elsewhere. Going from rank 2 to rank 3 doesn't just nudge your average result up a little; it hands you a whole extra die, which shifts both how high you can roll and how reliably you land near your average.</p>\n<p>As a guide for setting a DN:</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Difficulty</th><th>DN</th><th>This is roughly what a rank...</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Routine</td><td>6</td><td>...rank 2 skill clears comfortably, and even rank 1 has a real shot at</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Moderate</td><td>10</td><td>...rank 3 skill clears about 6 times in 10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hard</td><td>14</td><td>...rank 4 skill clears about half the time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very Hard</td><td>18</td><td>...rank 5 skill clears about half the time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extreme</td><td>22</td><td>...rank 6 skill clears less than half the time, even at the cap</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Worth Knowing: The Odds by Rank</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Because the dice add up instead of scaling a flat bonus, the odds move fast between ranks. Here's the actual success chance at each Difficulty, rank by rank:</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>| Rank | Dice | Routine (6) | Moderate (10) | Hard (14) | Very Hard (18) | Extreme (22) |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| 1 | 1d6 | 17% | 0% | 0% | 0% | 0% |</p><p>| 2 | 2d6 | 72% | 17% | 0% | 0% | 0% |</p><p>| 3 | 3d6 | 95% | 63% | 16% | 1% | 0% |</p><p>| 4 | 4d6 | ~100% | 90% | 56% | 16% | 1% |</p><p>| 5 | 5d6 | ~100% | 98% | 85% | 50% | 15% |</p><p>| 6 | 6d6 | ~100% | ~100% | 96% | 79% | 45% |</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>One thing worth knowing before you set a DN in play: at rank 1, anything harder than Routine is genuinely impossible, not just unlikely. A single d6 can't roll higher than 6, so a rank 1 skill can never reach a Moderate DN of 10 no matter how the dice land. That's a real consequence of adding dice together against a fixed number, not a rounding error, so keep it in mind when you're tempted to call for a Hard check from someone who's barely trained. If that hard floor feels wrong once you've played with it, the fix is to shift this whole table down rather than to raise it, since the ceiling side (ranks 5 and 6 struggling against Extreme) is doing what it should.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The GM doesn't need to call for a skill check every time a character does something related to a skill. If the task is simple enough to succeed automatically, just let it happen.</p>\n<h3>Helping Someone</h3>\n<p>Sometimes more than one Tamer wants to throw effort at the same task instead of just one person going it alone. When that happens, name one Tamer the Primary; theirs is the check that actually decides whether the task succeeds or fails. Everyone else pitching in is Helping.</p>\n<p>Each Helping Tamer rolls the same skill the Primary is using, but against a DN one tier lower than the Primary is facing (a Hard task becomes a Moderate check to help with, and so on). If a Helper meets or beats that lower DN, the Primary's own DN drops by 2 before they roll. If a Helper falls short, the Primary's DN rises by 2 instead; fumbling the help makes the job harder, not easier. Multiple Helpers stack, for better or worse.</p>\n<p>None of this changes whose roll actually decides the outcome. The task succeeds or fails entirely on the Primary Tamer's check against their DN, adjusted for whatever help they got. A Helper who completely blows their roll doesn't sink the task on its own if the Primary still clears their DN, and a Helper who succeeds cleanly doesn't guarantee anything either if the Primary still falls short.</p>\n<blockquote><p>The exact numbers here, a Helper DN one tier down and a swing of 2 per Helper, are a starting point picked to line up cleanly with the DN table above. Adjust them if they don't feel right once you've run it at the table.</p></blockquote>\n<h3>Skill EXP Costs</h3>\n<p>Skills cost less EXP to raise than Crest Stats, and the table now stops at rank 6 instead of rank 10:</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Upgrade</th><th>EXP Cost</th><th>Running Total</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 1 → 2</td><td>100</td><td>100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 2 → 3</td><td>150</td><td>250</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 3 → 4</td><td>200</td><td>450</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 4 → 5</td><td>250</td><td>700</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rank 5 → 6</td><td>300</td><td>1,000</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Remember that the Stat Cap Rule still applies on top of this cost. Paying the EXP for rank 6 doesn't do anything until the parent Crest Stat has also reached rank 9. You can bank the rank if you've got the EXP for it early, but it won't do anything for you until the stat catches up.</p>\n<h2>Looking Ahead</h2>\n<p>One more piece of a Tamer rounds out their sheet, and it's getting its own full chapter rather than a paragraph here.</p>\n<p><strong>Your Partner</strong> is that last piece: choosing your Digimon's line and building out their own sheet using their own separate 1,500 EXP. That starts in Chapter 7: Who Is Your Digimon?</p>\n<p>If you haven't spent any EXP toward a class yet, that's completely normal. Between your Crest Stat and your skills, the starting 1,500 EXP disappears fast. Classes are covered back in Chapter 4, and there's nothing wrong with entering play with EXP still unspent; you can put it toward a class the moment you've earned enough.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Example: Building a Tamer</h2>\n<p>Here's the whole process in one pass, using a Tamer named Kaito and his 1,500 starting EXP.</p>\n<p><strong>Who Are You.</strong> Kaito is 12, sharp-tongued, and more comfortable behind a screen than in a crowd. His Want is to prove he's useful without a keyboard in front of him; his Fear is being right about something and having nobody listen anyway. His Crest is Knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Crest Stats.</strong> Kaito puts 900 EXP into Knowledge, taking it from rank 1 to rank 4. That's the bulk of his budget gone, but it's also his Crest, and it raises the ceiling on every Knowledge skill he owns. He leaves his other five stats at rank 1 for now. 600 EXP remain.</p>\n<p>Because Kaito has earned 1,500 total EXP so far, his Hope Pool is 20, straight off the table in Chapter 5. It doesn't matter that most of it went into a single stat; total EXP earned is all that counts.</p>\n<p><strong>Skills.</strong> With Knowledge at rank 4, Kaito can raise any Knowledge skill as high as rank 4. He takes Decode to rank 3 (250 EXP) and Modify to rank 2 (100 EXP), spending 350 EXP and leaving 250 remaining.</p>\n<p><strong>Classes.</strong> 250 EXP isn't enough to open a class row from any book Kaito's group is using, and that's fine and expected at creation. He holds onto it. Thanks to the new Hope rule, saving it here costs him nothing.</p>\n<p><strong>Your Partner.</strong> Kaito's GM helps him settle on a Rookie-line partner suited to a Knowledge-leaning Tamer, and they move to Chapter 7 to finish building her.</p>\n<p>Kaito walks away from creation with Knowledge 4, Decode 3, Modify 2, everything else at rank 1, a Hope Pool of 20, 16 HP, and 250 EXP still sitting in reserve for whatever Session 1 throws at him.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":600,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb007whoismydigim7","name":"Chapter 7: Who Is Your Digimon?","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 7: Who Is Your Digimon?</h1>\n<p>Your Digimon partner is a full character, not a sidekick to your Tamer. They have their own personality, their own history, their own Crest Stats, and their own EXP pool. Everything from here through Chapter 8 is about giving that character the same weight on paper that your Tamer already has.</p>\n<h2>Choosing Your Starting Digimon</h2>\n<p>Work with your GM to pick your Digimon's starting Rookie form: a specific Digimon, like Agumon or Gabumon or Patamon, not an abstract archetype. That single choice is really all you need to make here. Everything downstream of it, Species Base in every Crest Stat (Chapter 8), Attribute, Element, and every Champion, Ultimate, and Mega form your partner can eventually reach, is already defined by that Digimon's own evolution line. You're not building a family tree from scratch; you're picking which existing one your partner belongs to, and the system handles the rest.</p>\n<p>Don't pick a starting form on stats alone. Consider what kind of personality this Digimon has, why they work well with your particular Tamer, and what the shape of that relationship actually looks like day to day. A Digimon chosen purely for a strong Species Base plays very differently at the table than one chosen because their whole personality clicks with what your Tamer needs.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Name</td><td>What does your Tamer call them?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Starting Digimon (Rookie Form)</td><td>The specific Digimon you're starting as, chosen with your GM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personality</td><td>Two or three words that sum them up</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Want</td><td>What does this Digimon want, beyond just growing stronger? Chapter 1 has a table of common answers if you want a starting point.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fear</td><td>What would actually shake them?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>History</td><td>How did they and their Tamer first meet?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Their Own EXP Pool</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Starting EXP Budget</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Your Digimon partner starts with the same 1,500 EXP as your Tamer, spent entirely separately.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>From here on, the two pools stay linked without ever touching. Every time your Tamer earns EXP, your Digimon earns the exact same amount into their own pool at the same moment (Chapter 9 covers how EXP gets awarded). Spending EXP on your Tamer never reduces what your Digimon has to spend, and spending on your Digimon never reduces your Tamer's pool. The two of you are always working with matching totals, even though what you've each bought with that total can look completely different.</p></blockquote>\n<p>With who your Digimon is settled, Chapter 8 covers what they can actually do: the four layers that build their Crest Stats, and how those stats turn into HP.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":700,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb008digimonstats8","name":"Chapter 8: Digimon Crest Stats","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Chapter 8: Digimon Crest Stats</h1>\n<p>Digimon use the same six Crest Stats as Tamers: Courage, Friendship, Love, Knowledge, Sincerity, and Reliability. What each one governs in combat is identical to what's described in Chapter 5; Courage still governs Hit Rate, Friendship still governs Speed, and so on down the line. What's different is how a Digimon's number in each stat actually gets built.</p>\n<h2>The Four Layer System</h2>\n<p>A Tamer's Crest Stat is a single number they raise directly. A Digimon's Crest Stat is four numbers stacked on top of each other:</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>The Four Layers</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Species Base.</strong> Set by the Digimon's species and current evolution stage. This is baked into the line you chose in Chapter 7, and it changes automatically every time your Digimon digivolves or dedigivolves.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Tamer Rank.</strong> Your own rank in that same Crest Stat, added straight across. This is the literal mechanical expression of the bond: raise your own Courage, and your partner's Courage rises with it, at no extra cost to either of you.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Digimon Invested.</strong> EXP your Digimon has spent from their own pool to raise that stat directly. This is the one layer that never resets, not on digivolution, not on dedigivolution, not ever.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Conditional.</strong> Temporary buffs and debuffs from the current fight. Cleared at the end of every encounter.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Add all four together and that's the number that actually shows up in combat. Take Agumon's Courage as an example: Species Base 4, his Tamer's Courage rank 3, and 2 more EXP-ranks his Tamer has spent directly on Agumon's own Courage. With no active Conditional modifiers, his total Courage is 4 + 3 + 2 + 0, or 9.</p>\n<p>That total, not any single layer of it, is what gets used everywhere a Digimon's Crest Stat matters, including the Hit Formula in Chapter 10.</p>\n<h2>Digimon HP</h2>\n<p>Your Digimon's Max HP is built from their total Sincerity, the same four-layer total described above:</p>\n<p><strong>Digimon Max HP = 20 + (total Sincerity × 4)</strong></p>\n<p>For example, a Digimon with a total Sincerity of 8 across all four layers has a Max HP of 20 + (8 × 4), or 52.</p>\n<h2>Digimon EXP Pool</h2>\n<p>Covered in Chapter 7: your Digimon's EXP pool always mirrors the total EXP your Tamer has earned, spent completely separately. Everything below assumes you're spending out of that pool.</p>\n<p>There are exactly two things a Digimon's own EXP can buy: raising their Crest Stats directly (the Digimon Invested layer, right below), and unlocking additional Digivolution forms (further down this chapter). Skills work differently for a Digimon than they do for a Tamer; see Digimon Skills at the end of this chapter for why.</p>\n<h2>Raising Digimon Stats</h2>\n<p>Species Base and Tamer Rank take care of themselves; Digimon Invested is the layer you buy up directly, and each rank costs more than the last:</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Upgrade</th><th>EXP Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0 → 1</td><td>100 EXP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 → 2</td><td>200 EXP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 → 3</td><td>300 EXP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 → 4</td><td>400 EXP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>And so on</td><td>Rank × 100 EXP per step</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Digivolution Form Unlocks</h2>\n<p>Reaching a new stage doesn't automatically hand you every Digimon that stage could become. Your Digimon's own EXP pool gates how many alternate forms at each stage you can actually unlock:</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Min Digimon EXP</th><th>First Form</th><th>Each Additional Form</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rookie</td><td>Free</td><td>Free</td><td>50 EXP per form</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Champion</td><td>2,000 EXP</td><td>Free</td><td>100 EXP per form</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ultimate</td><td>8,000 EXP</td><td>Free</td><td>300 EXP per form</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mega</td><td>20,000 EXP</td><td>Free</td><td>500 EXP per form</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Digivolution Path Rule</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>A Digimon can only digivolve into a form its current stage actually leads to. Owning an unlocked form through some other path doesn't help if there's no valid line connecting your Digimon's current form to it. If Agumon has Garurumon unlocked through an alternate path, he still can't digivolve into Garurumon, because Agumon's own line simply doesn't go there. The path has to be valid from your current form, not just unlocked somewhere in your pool.</p></blockquote>\n<h2>Digimon Skills</h2>\n<p>Digimon don't buy their own skill ranks the way Tamers do. When a Digimon needs to make a skill check, whether that's a Tame check to bond with a wild Digital creature, a Trace check to follow a scent, or an Archive check to recall something about the Digital World, they roll their Tamer's rank in that same skill, not a rank of their own.</p>\n<p>If a Tamer's Trace is rank 3, their Digimon's Trace checks also roll 3d6 and get summed against the DN exactly like the Tamer's own would; Chapter 6 covers the full mechanic. Raise your own skills, and your partner's competence at the same things rises right alongside you, the same way your Crest Stats already feed their combat math above.</p>\n<p>This is why a Digimon's own EXP pool never goes toward skills at all. Everything they can spend on themselves goes to exactly two things, both covered above: raising their Crest Stats directly through the Digimon Invested layer, and unlocking additional Digivolution forms at each stage. There's no separate skill list or skill-purchase table for a Digimon to work through here; their skills are whatever their Tamer's sheet already says.</p>\n<p>With a Digimon's stats, HP, and skills all settled, Chapter 11: Attacks & Tags covers the last piece of their sheet: the moves they actually fight with.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":800,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb009growthexp009","name":"Chapter 9: Growth & Experience","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 9: Growth & Experience</strong></h1>\n<p>There are no levels in Tamer's Bond. Characters grow by spending EXP. Every purchase is a tradeoff.</p>\n<h2><strong>Earning EXP</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Event</strong></th><th><strong>EXP Reward</strong></th><th><strong>Notes</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Short or light session</td><td>75–150</td><td>Mostly roleplay, travel, or downtime</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standard session</td><td>200–400</td><td>A mix of combat, exploration, and story</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heavy or climactic session</td><td>400–600</td><td>Boss fights, major revelations, high stakes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Story milestone</td><td>500–1,000</td><td>Defeating a major villain, finding a crest, arc resolution</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personal character moment</td><td>100–300</td><td>Awarded individually for standout roleplay or growth</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>How EXP Pools Work</strong> Tamers and Digimon each have their own separate EXP pool. They do not share one pool. Both pools always contain the same total EXP earned. Example: The GM awards 350 EXP. The Tamer's pool gains 350. The Digimon's pool ALSO gains 350 independently. Spending EXP on the Tamer does NOT reduce the Digimon's pool and vice versa.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Campaign Pacing</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Campaign Stage</strong></th><th><strong>Approx. Total EXP</strong></th><th><strong>Expected State</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Session 1 (creation)</td><td>1,500</td><td>Stats at 1–3, a few skills at 2–3, clearly defined but fragile</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Season 1 midpoint</td><td>8,000–10,000</td><td>Primary stat pushing rank 4–5, core skills at 3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Season 1 end</td><td>15,000–20,000</td><td>Primary stat at 5–6, secondary stats 3–4, first classes purchased</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Season 2 end</td><td>35,000–45,000</td><td>Primary stat pushing rank 8, skills nearing rank 5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Full completion</td><td>~70,000+</td><td>All stats and skills maxed, all desired classes purchased</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":900,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb010combat000010","name":"Chapter 10: Combat","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 10: Combat</strong></h1>\n<p>Combat is where everything else in this book gets put to the test. Every stat you've raised, every skill you've trained, every point of Hope you've earned, all of it comes down to the moment a fight actually starts. This chapter walks through how a fight actually runs: how turn order gets decided, what a character can do on their turn, and the exact math behind every attack that lands or misses.</p>\n<p>None of this happens in isolation. Every roll in this chapter ties straight back to the Crest Stats and skills your Tamer and Digimon already have, so if something here feels unfamiliar, it's worth checking back on the stat or skill behind it.</p>\n<h2><strong>The Round Structure</strong></h2>\n<p>Combat takes place on a grid where 1 square = 1 space. Both Tamers and Digimon take their own separate turns in initiative order; Digimon are the primary fighters, but Tamers can fight too.</p>\n<p>Each round of combat follows this order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Determine Turn Order. Digimon use Friendship (no multiplier). Tamers use Friendship x2. Highest acts first. Ties broken by higher raw Friendship stat.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Each Character Takes Their Turn. In initiative order, each Tamer and each Digimon takes their full turn before the next character acts.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Resolution Stage. CHARGE moves fire. Status effects tick. Lingering conditions resolve.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2><strong>Holding Your Action</strong></h2>\n<p>At the start of your turn, before you do anything else, you may choose to hold your action instead of acting right away. Pick another character who hasn't taken their turn yet this round, and you take your turn immediately after them instead of now.</p>\n<p>Holding only ever moves you later, never back. Once you've held, that choice is locked in: if another character also holds, or the fight shifts in some way that makes you wish you'd acted sooner, you can't jump back into the spot you gave up or any point earlier than wherever you land now. The only direction a held action can move is forward.</p>\n<p>Each character can hold their action once per round. Once you've held and then taken your turn, you're done, you can't hold again later in the same round.</p>\n<h2><strong>Actions on Your Turn</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Action Commitment Rule</strong> Once a character begins an action, they must finish it before starting another. Movement cannot be split around other actions. You may take your Move Action, Basic Action, and Digivolving Action in whatever order you like, but each one must be completed before the next begins.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Action Type</strong></th><th><strong>What It Covers</strong></th><th><strong>Limit</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Move Action</td><td>Move up to your full movement, or dedigivolve instead. See the Move Actions chart below.</td><td>Once per round</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Basic Action</td><td>The main thing you do: attacking, using an item, or a special action. See the Basic Actions chart below.</td><td>Once per round</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Digivolving Action</td><td>A Tamer attempts to digivolve their partner. Only usable on the Tamer's own turn, and only if the Tamer has their Digivice with them.</td><td>Once per round, Tamer's turn only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Free Action</td><td>Minor things that take no meaningful time. Most Free Actions are gained through Classes; see the Free Actions chart below for the ones every character starts with.</td><td>Once per round</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>At the start of each Tamer's turn, not the start of the round, deduct the Hope cost for their Digimon's current digivolution stage. If Hope hits 0 the Digimon immediately reverts to Default Stage before any actions.</p>\n<h3><strong>Move Actions</strong></h3>\n<p>When moving, you must finish your Move Action before starting another action. You can't split your movement around something else.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Move Action</strong></th><th><strong>Effect</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Move</td><td>Move up to your full movement. Digimon move a number of spaces equal to their Friendship stat; Tamers move double that, Friendship x2.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dedigivolve</td><td>Instead of moving, voluntarily drop your Digimon one or two stages. Hope cost adjusts to the new stage before it's spent.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Digivice</td><td>Instead of moving, use your Digivice to perform one of its actions. Every Digivice grants its own action; check Chapter 15: Items for what your specific model can do.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Dedigivolution Clarification</strong> Both the Tamer AND the Digimon can each trigger a voluntary dedigivolution on their own turn using their own Move Action. They cannot both dedigivolve in the same round, each can only use their own Move Action once. Maximum voluntary dedigivolution per turn is 2 stages, if either the Tamer or Digimon uses the two-stage option. Status effects are always cleared on any digivolution or dedigivolution, voluntary or forced.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Basic Actions</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Basic Action</strong></th><th><strong>How It Works</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Attack</td><td>Use one of your active moves against a target. Follows the full attack sequence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sprint</td><td>Move a second time, up to your full movement. Uses your Basic Action for the turn.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use Item</td><td>Use a carried item. Effect depends on the item.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Skill Check</td><td>Make any relevant skill check the situation calls for.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Push Through (Tamer only)</td><td>Once an encounter make a skill check against DN 5, using a skill linked to the Crest you chose in character building; choose Love as your Crest and the check has to use a Love skill, and so on. If you meet or beat the DN, restore Hope equal to the number you rolled. This Hope goes only to the Tamer who used the action. Once per encounter. Cannot be used if your Hope is already at maximum.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Free Actions</strong></h3>\n<p>Most Free Actions are gained through Classes rather than starting on every sheet. A Free Action doesn't have to happen on your turn, but you only get one per round no matter when you use it: if you already spent it off-turn before your turn came up, you can't take another one when your turn arrives.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Free Action</strong></th><th><strong>Effect</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Call Out</td><td>Your partner gains +1 to their next attack roll, lasting until the start of your next turn.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Making an Attack: Step by Step</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Adjacent</strong> Adjacent means any of the 8 squares surrounding a character's space, including diagonals. This applies to all [MELEE] moves and any ability that references adjacency.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Step 1: Select Target and Move</strong></h3>\n<p>Choose the target and which of your four active moves you are using. The move must be usable at your current stage. Check the move's tags for range or area restrictions. Remember: once you begin your movement, complete it before taking your Basic Action.</p>\n<h3><strong>Step 2: Determine Hit or Miss</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Hit Formula</strong> Roll: 1d20 + Attacker's total Courage stat, VS Target: Defender's total Reliability stat + 10 Meet or beat the target number = HIT Fall short = MISS, attack ends here, no damage</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Roll Result</strong></th><th><strong>Effect</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Natural 20</td><td>CRITICAL HIT: automatic hit. Double the final damage result after all calculations.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Natural 1</td><td>CRITICAL FAIL: automatic miss. Attack ends immediately. No damage.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Step 3: Determine Damage</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Damage Formula</strong> Damage = PR dice + Attacker's total Knowledge - Defender's total Love. PR dice is determined by the move's Power Rating (see Chapter 11: Attacks & Tags). Minimum damage before multipliers: 1.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Step 4: Apply Weakness or Strength</strong></h3>\n<p>Apply attribute and element multipliers. Calculate attribute first then multiply by element.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Attacker Attribute</strong></th><th><strong>Defender Attribute</strong></th><th><strong>Multiplier</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Vaccine</td><td>Virus</td><td>x2.0 advantage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Virus</td><td>Data</td><td>x2.0 advantage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Data</td><td>Vaccine</td><td>x2.0 advantage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vaccine</td><td>Data</td><td>x0.5 disadvantage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Data</td><td>Virus</td><td>x0.5 disadvantage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Virus</td><td>Vaccine</td><td>x0.5 disadvantage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Free</td><td>Any</td><td>x1.0 always neutral</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unknown</td><td>Any</td><td>x2.0 beats all attributes</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Element</strong></th><th><strong>Resistant To</strong></th><th><strong>Weak To</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fire</td><td>Fire (x0.5)</td><td>Water, Earth (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Water</td><td>Water (x0.5)</td><td>Electric, Plant (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plant</td><td>Plant (x0.5)</td><td>Fire, Wind (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electric</td><td>Electric (x0.5)</td><td>Earth, Wind (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wind</td><td>Wind (x0.5)</td><td>Electric, Fire (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Earth</td><td>Earth (x0.5)</td><td>Water, Plant (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Light</td><td>Light (x0.5)</td><td>Dark (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dark</td><td>Dark (x0.5)</td><td>Light (x1.5)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neutral</td><td>Nothing</td><td>Nothing, always x1.0</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Step 5: Apply Damage</strong></h3>\n<p>Subtract the final damage total from the target's current Health. If the target's Health is 0 or below, they're defeated and can't take any more actions for the rest of this combat encounter.</p>\n<h2><strong>Ending the Fight</strong></h2>\n<p>A single defeat doesn't end an encounter on its own. Combat is actually over once one side is the only one left with anyone able to act, every character on the other side has been defeated, fled, or otherwise dropped out of the fight, or once one side concedes outright, whether that's a wild Digimon breaking off and running, an enemy Tamer calling it, or your own party deciding the smart move is pulling out rather than pushing a fight you can't win. Chapter 14: Defeat, Victory & Tamer Conditions covers what actually happens to a character once they've been taken out, and what a party walks away with once the dust settles.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1000,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb011attackstags11","name":"Chapter 11: Attacks & Tags","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 11: Attacks & Tags</strong></h1>\n<p>A Digimon does not simply attack, it expresses itself. Every move is a piece of their history, a technique carried forward from some stage of their evolution, whether that's the signature move that woke up the moment they first reached a new stage or one of the techniques they picked back up at their last long rest. This chapter covers how those moves actually function at the table: the tags that define what each one can do, the Power Rating that sets how hard it hits, and the rules for figuring out which moves in a Digimon's history are actually usable right now.</p>\n<h2><strong>The Basic Attack</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Basic Attack</strong> Element: Neutral   PR: 2 (1d6)   Tag: [MELEE] Available to: All Tamers and Digimon at all times. The Basic Attack works exactly like any other attack: it follows the full attack sequence using the normal hit roll, damage formula, and all applicable multipliers. It does not occupy any of the four active move slots. It is simply always there. Because it has [MELEE], the attacker must be adjacent (one of the 8 surrounding squares) to their target.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Power Rating (PR) Dice Table</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>PR</strong></th><th><strong>Dice</strong></th><th><strong>Avg</strong></th><th><strong>Typical Stage</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>1d4</td><td>2.5</td><td>In-Training</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>1d6</td><td>3.5</td><td>Rookie weak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>1d8</td><td>4.5</td><td>Rookie standard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>1d10</td><td>5.5</td><td>Rookie strong</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>1d12</td><td>6.5</td><td>Champion weak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>2d6</td><td>7</td><td>Champion standard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>2d8</td><td>9</td><td>Champion strong</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>2d10</td><td>11</td><td>Ultimate weak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>9</td><td>2d12</td><td>13</td><td>Ultimate standard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10</td><td>3d8</td><td>13.5</td><td>Ultimate strong / Mega weak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11</td><td>3d10</td><td>16.5</td><td>Mega standard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12</td><td>3d12</td><td>19.5</td><td>Mega strong</td></tr>\n<tr><td>13</td><td>4d10</td><td>22</td><td>Mega II standard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>14</td><td>4d12</td><td>26</td><td>Mega II strong</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>5d10</td><td>27.5</td><td>Mega II signature (maximum)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Mega II: Stage Note</strong> Mega II is not a separate mechanical stage. In all rules, Mega II Digimon are treated as Mega stage. Some Mega-stage Digimon are simply able to digivolve into another Mega-stage form. The PR 13–15 range exists to represent these top-tier forms without changing how the stage rules work. Fresh-stage Digimon have no moves and no PR, they are narrative only.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Move Tags</strong></h2>\n<h3><strong>Delivery Tags</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Tag</strong></th><th><strong>Effect</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>[MELEE]</td><td>Must be adjacent (any of the 8 surrounding squares) to the target to use this move.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[RANGE X]</td><td>Can hit targets up to X spaces away.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[PIERCE]</td><td>Ignores the target's Love stat damage reduction entirely.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[TRUE]</td><td>Cannot miss if the hit roll succeeds. A natural 1 still misses, but instead of dealing no damage, the damage is calculated as normal and then halved before it's applied.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Area Tags</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Tag</strong></th><th><strong>Effect</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>[BURST X]</td><td>Hits all enemies within X spaces of the ATTACKER. Roll damage once and apply to all targets.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[BLAST X]</td><td>Hits all enemies within X spaces of the TARGET. Roll damage once and apply to all targets.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[CHAIN X,Y]</td><td>Bounces from the primary target to X additional targets each within Y spaces. Each subsequent target takes half the damage of the previous.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Timing Tags</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Tag</strong></th><th><strong>Effect</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>[CHARGE]</td><td>Costs your Basic Action this turn. The move fires at the Resolution Stage at the end of the round. You may still move normally.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[COUNTER]</td><td>Can only be used as a Free Action immediately after this character was attacked, only if they have not used their Free Action this round.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[RUSH]</td><td>Does not consume your Move Action. Move up to your full speed (Digimon = Friendship spaces, Tamer = Friendship x2 spaces) and attack at any point during that movement. You may still take your Move Action separately before or after using RUSH.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3><strong>Status Effect Tags</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Tag</strong></th><th><strong>Rules</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>[BURN X,Y]</td><td>On hit, target gains a Burn stack. X = damage taken at start of target's turn. Y = number of ticks. If hit with BURN again: take the higher X value and add the Y counters together.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[FREEZE]</td><td>Target cannot act. At end of their turn roll 1d20 + total Love vs DN 14 to break. Also breaks immediately if the target takes any damage.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[PARALYZE X]</td><td>Target's Speed is halved. X = stacks. At start of target's turn lose 1 stack. 1–3 stacks: choose Basic OR Move Action. 4+ stacks: cannot act. Natural 20 on any roll clears all stacks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[BLIND]</td><td>On hit, target gains 2 Blind stacks. Target suffers -4 to all attack hit rolls while Blind.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[CONFUSE]</td><td>At start of the target's turn, roll 1d20 + total Friendship vs DN 14. Fail = must attack the closest ally.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[DRAIN]</td><td>On hit, the attacker regains HP equal to half the damage dealt (round down, minimum 1).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[PUSH]</td><td>On hit, push the target away. Spaces pushed = Attacker's Courage minus Defender's Courage. Minimum 1 space.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[POISON X]</td><td>On a natural attack roll of 15 or higher, the target is Poisoned with X stacks. If already Poisoned, add X to their current Poison total. At the start of their turn the target takes damage equal to their current Poison stacks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[SLEEP]</td><td>Target cannot act. At the start of their turn they make a Firewall skill check DN 13, on a success they wake and may act normally this turn. They also wake immediately if they take any damage.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[RECOVERY]</td><td>Instead of dealing damage, this move automatically restores HP to its target equal to the move's PR dice roll. No hit roll is required.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[FRAGMENT X]</td><td>On hit, target gains X Fragment stacks. If already Fragmented, add X to their current stack total. At the start of the target's turn, lose 1 stack. While Fragmented, a Digimon cannot regain HP from any source, including [RECOVERY], items, or skill checks.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Status Effect Decay</strong> At the start of a Digimon's turn, before any other effects apply, it loses 1 stack of every stacking status effect currently affecting it.</th></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Status Effects and Digivolution</strong> All active status effects, including Poison and Sleep, are cleared immediately whenever a Digimon digivolves, dedigivolves, or sidedigivolves, whether the change was voluntary or forced.</th></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Status Effects and Encounters All active status effects, including Poison and Fragment, are automatically cleared the instant an encounter ends, no matter how it was resolved.</p>\n<h2><strong>How Moves Are Learned</strong></h2>\n<p>Every Digimon has a Signature Move tied to their current stage. The moment your Digimon digivolves into a stage for the first time, that stage's Signature Move unlocks and gets added to their Move Pool for good; dedigivolving afterward doesn't take it back out. Your Digimon's active Signature Move in combat is always whichever one matches their current stage, but every Signature Move they've unlocked along the way stays sitting in the Move Pool for later.</p>\n<p>The Move Pool only grows. Every new stage a Digimon reaches adds that stage's Signature Move to the pool, and nothing gets removed from it just because a Digimon dedigivolves.</p>\n<p>At a long rest, a Digimon may look through their whole Move Pool and choose 3 moves to carry as their Selected Moves, on top of whatever their current Signature Move happens to be.</p>\n<p>A Digimon can only actually use a Selected Move or their Signature Move if their current stage is equal to or higher than the stage that move was learned at. A move learned at Champion can't be used while the Digimon is sitting at Rookie, even if it's already picked as a Selected Move; it simply comes back online the next time they digivolve back up to Champion or beyond.</p>\n<p>Digimon always have access to their Basic Attack, no matter their current stage or which moves they've selected.</p>\n<p>Between the Basic Attack, a Signature Move that grows with every stage a Digimon reaches, and three Selected Moves refreshed at every long rest, a Digimon's kit is never really finished. It's worth revisiting the Move Pool every time a Digimon hits a new stage: an old move that's been sitting dormant can suddenly be worth carrying again, and a newer one might open up an entirely different way to fight than whatever's currently selected.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1100,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb012digivolution2","name":"Chapter 12: Digivolution","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 12: Digivolution</strong></h1>\n<p>Digivolution is not a button you press. It is a question your partner asks of you: do you have enough left? Every evolution is paid for in Hope, and Hope is not infinite.</p>\n<h2><strong>Stages</strong></h2>\n<p>A Digimon moves through several distinct stages over the course of its life, and as a general rule, the higher the stage, the stronger the Digimon standing in it. Every stage brings a real jump in what a Digimon can do, not just a bigger number on the sheet.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Stage</strong></th><th><strong>Notes</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fresh</td><td>No stats, no moves. Narrative only.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In-Training</td><td>All base stats = 1. PR 1 signature move only.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rookie</td><td>Starting stage for all new partners. PR 2–4.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Champion</td><td>First major digivolution milestone. PR 5–7.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ultimate</td><td>Mid-campaign goal. PR 8–10.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mega</td><td>Campaign endgame target. PR 10–12. Some Mega Digimon can digivolve into another Mega form (PR 13–15).</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Hope, Digivolution Fuel</strong></h2>\n<p>Hope is what fuels every digivolution and keeps a Digimon holding a stage above Default. Chapter 5 covers exactly how your Hope Pool is sized, off your total EXP earned over your whole career, not your current stats or any single Crest Stat's rank. What matters here is what that Hope actually gets spent on once you have it.</p>\n<p>Hope fully restores whenever you rest.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Stage</strong></th><th><strong>Hope Cost Per Turn</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>In-Training</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rookie</td><td>3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Champion</td><td>6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ultimate</td><td>10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mega</td><td>15</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Stacking Costs</strong> When digivolved multiple stages above Default Stage, pay the SUM of all stage costs above Default. Example: Default = Rookie, Current = Ultimate, Cost = Champion (6) + Ultimate (10) = 16 Hope per Tamer turn</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Default Stage, Current Stage, and Max Default Stage</strong></h2>\n<p>Three different terms describe where a Digimon actually stands at any given moment, and it's worth being clear on all three before getting into how digivolving actually works turn to turn.</p>\n<p>Default Stage is where a Digimon rests when nothing is pushing them higher or lower. For a brand new partner, that's Rookie.</p>\n<p>Current Stage is simply wherever the Digimon actually sits right now, this exact moment. It can match their Default Stage, or sit higher or lower than it, depending on what's happened so far this encounter.</p>\n<p>Max Default Stage is the highest a Digimon's Default Stage is ever allowed to climb to over the course of a campaign. By default, that ceiling is Ultimate. A Digimon's Default Stage can rise through Daily Rest Recovery (covered further down this chapter) all the way up to Ultimate on its own, but pushing that ceiling any higher, into Mega, takes a class ability or a story-driven exception.</p>\n<p>None of this changes the separate rule that a Digimon can digivolve up to 2 stages above whatever their Default Stage happens to be at any given time. That ceiling moves with your Default Stage; it isn't locked to any one absolute stage. With a Default Stage of Rookie, the highest reachable stage is Ultimate, 2 stages up. If a Digimon's Default Stage ever climbs to Champion instead, whether through Daily Rest Recovery, a class ability, or a story reason, the highest reachable stage automatically becomes Mega, 2 stages up from there.</p>\n<p>The ceiling is always measured from Default Stage, never from wherever a Digimon's Current Stage happens to sit in the moment. Say a Digimon's Default Stage is Rookie, and their Tamer dedigivolved them down to In-Training earlier this fight. From In-Training, they can still digivolve all the way up to Ultimate, three steps up from where they're currently sitting, because the ceiling is still counting from Default Stage (Rookie), not from Current Stage (In-Training).</p>\n<h2><strong>Digivolving</strong></h2>\n<p>Digivolving moves a Digimon one stage higher. It uses the Tamer's Digivolving Action on their own turn, and it can only be used once per round. You can only climb one stage at a time unless a class ability specifically says otherwise, and you can never push a Digimon's Current Stage more than 2 stages above their Default Stage.</p>\n<p>A Digimon can only digivolve into a form its current form actually has a valid path to. Simply having a form unlocked somewhere in your pool isn't enough. If a Rookie has two different Champion forms unlocked but only one of them is reachable from its current species line, that's the only one available to digivolve into right now.</p>\n<p>Digivolving to a higher stage also increases the Hope cost you pay each turn to hold that stage, since the Hope Cost Per Turn table above only climbs as the stage does.</p>\n<h2><strong>Dedigivolving</strong></h2>\n<p>Dedigivolving drops a Digimon one or two stages lower. It uses a Move Action, either the Tamer's or the Digimon's own, though only one of the two can use theirs in a given round.</p>\n<p>Dedigivolving isn't simply the reverse of digivolving into whatever lower-stage form you like. A Digimon can only dedigivolve back into the specific form it actually used to reach its current stage, not just any unlocked form that happens to sit at that lower stage. Several different In-Training Digimon might all be able to digivolve into Agumon, but if this particular Tamer's partner reached Agumon by digivolving from Koromon specifically, dedigivolving only ever takes them back to Koromon. Even if Budmon is also unlocked and also leads to Agumon on paper, it was never actually the form this Digimon used to get there, so it isn't available to dedigivolve into.</p>\n<p>Dedigivolving to a lower stage reduces the Hope cost you pay each turn to maintain it, for the same reason in reverse.</p>\n<h2><strong>Sidedigivolving</strong></h2>\n<p>Sidedigivolving swaps a Digimon into a different unlocked form at the same stage they're currently at, without moving up or down at all. It uses the Tamer's Digivolving Action, the same as digivolving up a stage.</p>\n<p>A Digimon can only sidedigivolve into a form that shares the same previous form as the one they're currently in, and that form has to already be unlocked and gained by the Digimon. Koromon can digivolve into both Betamon and Agumon. If this Tamer's partner is currently Agumon, having come from Koromon, they can sidedigivolve over to Betamon instead, since Betamon also digivolves from that same Koromon. What that partner can't do is sidedigivolve into some other Rookie form that didn't come from Koromon, even if it happens to be unlocked somewhere else in the pool.</p>\n<p>Since Sidedigivolving never changes what stage a Digimon is at, it doesn't change the Hope cost paid each turn either.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Status Effects and Stage Changes</strong> All active status effects clear immediately whenever a Digimon changes stage or form this way, whether that's digivolving, dedigivolving, or sidedigivolving, voluntary or forced.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Forcing a Digivolution</strong></h2>\n<p>When a Tamer digivolves their partner, they choose how much Hope to spend to sustain that stage: anywhere from the full Hope Cost Per Turn required, down to 0. Paying the full amount keeps the digivolution completely stable from that point on. Paying anything less is forcing it, and forcing risks the digivolution going dark instead.</p>\n<p>Roll d100 the moment you digivolve. Roll ABOVE the threshold below and the digivolution holds cleanly, at whatever reduced spend rate you chose. That rate becomes your ongoing Hope cost per turn for as long as your Digimon holds this stage. Roll at or under the threshold and the Digimon dark digivolves instead (see Dark Digivolution, further down this chapter).</p>\n<p><strong>Threshold = (1 - Hope Spent / Full Cost) x Stage Danger</strong></p>\n<p>Stage Danger values: Rookie 20, Champion 35, Ultimate 55, Mega 80.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Worth Knowing: Dark Digivolving Chance by Stage</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>That formula is easiest to read as a straight percentage chance of going dark, since a d100 roll at or under the threshold triggers it:</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>| Hope Spent (% of Full Cost) | Rookie | Champion | Ultimate | Mega |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| 100% (full cost) | 0% | 0% | 0% | 0% |</p><p>| 75% | 5% | 9% | 14% | 20% |</p><p>| 50% | 10% | 18% | 28% | 40% |</p><p>| 25% | 15% | 26% | 41% | 60% |</p><p>| 0% (free) | 20% | 35% | 55% | 80% |</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Paying nothing at all is never a guaranteed dark digivolution at any stage, but it gets a lot less safe the higher the stage climbs. Sustaining a Rookie for free only risks going dark 1 time in 5. Doing the same at Mega fails more often than it works.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Out of combat you must always pay the full Hope cost for whatever stage you're holding. Forcing, spending less than full and risking a dark digivolution, is only ever available in combat.</p>\n<h2><strong>HP on Digivolution</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Action</strong></th><th><strong>HP Rule</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Digivolving up</td><td>Current HP increases by the difference between old and new max HP.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dedigivolving</td><td>Current HP is capped at the new lower max HP.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Out-of-combat healing</td><td>Valid strategy: digivolve up then dedigivolve to bank HP gain. Must pay full Hope cost. No forcing out of combat.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Hitting 0 HP: The Cascade</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Situation</strong></th><th><strong>What Happens</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Above Default Stage</td><td>Revert to Default Stage at 1 HP. Still in the fight.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>At Default Stage</td><td>Default Stage drops by 1. Remain at 1 HP.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overkill hit (>50% max HP)</td><td>Default Stage drops by 1 additionally. Stacks with the above.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Egg state</td><td>If Default Stage cannot drop further, become an egg. Out until next session.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Dark Digivolution</strong></h2>\n<p>When a forced digivolution roll fails, the Digimon doesn't just fail to hold its stage; it goes dark instead. The GM chooses which dark form actually emerges.</p>\n<p>A dark form costs nothing to maintain. It holds itself with no Hope spent per turn at all, and it doesn't revert early no matter what happens, staying dark until the encounter itself ends. While dark, the GM controls the Digimon directly: it attacks whoever is closest, Tamer or Digimon alike, and favors targets it holds a type advantage against. The dark form's signature move stays with the Digimon for good once the encounter ends, permanently added to its Move Pool alongside everything else it's unlocked. The dark state itself only ends when the encounter does, whether that's every enemy defeated, the dark Digimon defeated, or everyone else choosing to submit.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Submitting to a Dark Digimon</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>Facing a dark Digimon, a Tamer can choose to submit rather than keep fighting it. Submitting pulls both the Tamer and their partner out of the encounter entirely; neither one can be targeted by the dark Digimon anymore. The cost is steep: the moment a Tamer submits, their Hope drops straight to 0.</p></blockquote>\n<p>></p>\n<blockquote><p>If the dark Digimon a Tamer is submitting to is their own partner, only the Tamer leaves the encounter this way. Their Digimon stays in the fight, still under the GM's control until the encounter ends, since it isn't answering to its Tamer anymore anyway.</p></blockquote>\n<h2><strong>Daily Rest Recovery</strong></h2>\n<p>After each full day of rest, if the Digimon's Default Stage is below their Max Default Stage, it increases by one. Default Stage cannot exceed Max Default Stage through any means.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1200,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb013resting00013","name":"Chapter 13: Resting & Encounters","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 13: Resting & Encounters</strong></h1>\n<h2><strong>What Is an Encounter?</strong></h2>\n<p>An encounter is a defined period of dramatic activity: a fight, a dangerous puzzle, a tense negotiation, or any situation where the GM calls for structured turns and meaningful stakes. Dark Digivolution ends at the end of each encounter. Conditional stat modifiers clear at the end of each encounter too.</p>\n<h2><strong>Time</strong></h2>\n<p>Time doesn't move at the same rate in the Human World and the Digital World, and how the two line up is entirely up to the setting. Some campaigns run the two worlds on completely different clocks: weeks can pass in the Digital World while only minutes go by back home. Others keep the two worlds synced exactly, a minute spent in the Digital World is a minute spent in the Human World too. Neither approach is more correct than the other; it's a setting decision the GM makes for their own campaign, the same as anything else about how the two worlds relate.</p>\n<p>The Digital World doesn't even have to be consistent with itself. Some settings have a Digital World where the sun never moves, stuck at the same point in the sky no matter how long anyone spends there. Others run the opposite way, locked into permanent night. The Digital World isn't bound to behave like a normal world at all, and a GM is free to have how time passes there shift from one hour to the next if that's what the setting calls for.</p>\n<h2><strong>Resting and Adventuring</strong></h2>\n<p>As a baseline, a party should rest for 10 hours for every 14 hours they spend adventuring. Resting is where the party cooks, sleeps, and tends to their wounds.</p>\n<p>When the party rests, both Tamer and Digimon restore their HP fully, and every Tamer's Hope refills to its max.</p>\n<p>If a rest gets interrupted partway through, the party still gets that HP and Hope restoration, but they'll need to finish out the rest itself at the next opportunity they get.</p>\n<p>Feeding both a Tamer and their Digimon during a rest takes 2 portions of food, one for each, to keep their Hope Pool at full strength.</p>\n<h3><strong>Scavenging</strong></h3>\n<p>Once per rest, a Tamer may attempt a Field Ops check against DN 10 to scavenge for food. On a success, they find enough to feed themselves and their own Digimon for that rest, though not the rest of the party; any other Tamer who wants to feed their own pair this way needs to make their own attempt. Food found by scavenging can't be saved for later. Whatever isn't eaten during that same rest simply disappears.</p>\n<p>If the party misses a rest, misses a meal, or both, their Hope Pool drops by the amount below:</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Missed Rests or Meals</th><th>Effect on Hope Pool</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0 (rested and fed)</td><td>Normal, no penalty</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Hope pool halved</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Hope pool quartered</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Hope pool reduced to one eighth</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1300,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb014defeatvict14","name":"Chapter 14: Defeat, Victory & Tamer Conditions","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 14: Defeat, Victory & Tamer Conditions</strong></h1>\n<p>Loss is part of Digimon, not a failure state to be avoided at all costs. If every encounter ends in a clean win, characters never really get tested, and growth starts to feel automatic instead of earned. If every encounter ends in a loss, the story stalls and nobody wants to keep playing. This chapter covers both directions: what actually happens when a fight goes well, and just as importantly, what happens when it doesn't, so a loss becomes a turning point in the story instead of a dead end at the table.</p>\n<h2><strong>Winning an Encounter</strong></h2>\n<p>An encounter is won when all opposing forces are defeated, driven off, or choose to submit. The GM declares the encounter over. All conditional stat modifiers clear, and Dark Digivolution ends for anyone still holding it. The party may rest, loot, or press on.</p>\n<h2><strong>Losing an Encounter</strong></h2>\n<p>An encounter is lost when every Digimon has reverted to egg state and every Tamer is at 0 HP, or when the party chooses to submit outright. From there it's up to the GM to decide what happens next: the enemy might capture the party, leave them where they fell, or simply move on. EXP is still awarded even after a loss. The party learned something from the fight, even if all they learned was exactly how strong the enemy really is.</p>\n<h2><strong>Tamer HP and Defeat</strong></h2>\n<p>If a Tamer reaches 0 HP during an encounter, they can't take any actions for the rest of it. They're still present, but they can't attack, use items, digivolve their partner, or make skill checks.</p>\n<p>Once the battle is over, everyone is restored to a minimum of 1 HP. If a Tamer ends the fight sitting at 1 HP this way, they pick up the same Hope penalty a missed rest or meal carries (see the chart in Chapter 13), and it sticks around until they actually get to rest.</p>\n<h2><strong>Total Party Defeat</strong></h2>\n<p>When every Tamer and every Digimon in the party goes down, the GM decides what happens next: capture, a retreat by the enemy, an intervention from an NPC, or something else entirely. Every Digimon in the party stays in egg state until the party's next rest, or until a class ability intervenes first. Every Tamer recovers to 1 HP once the encounter resolves and picks up the same Hope penalty as ending a fight at 1 HP normally would (see the chart in Chapter 13), lasting until they actually get to rest. EXP is still awarded all the same.</p>\n<p>Defeat is not a punishment. It is a turning point. The story continues, and what the party does next is entirely up to them.</p>\n<p>None of this is meant to feel like a penalty for playing the game honestly. A Tamer at 0 HP, a Digimon back in its egg, even a full party defeat, all of it exists to keep the story moving forward rather than ending it there. The dice decide how a fight goes. They don't decide whether the campaign keeps going.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1400,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb015items0000015","name":"Chapter 15: Items","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 15: Items</strong></h1>\n<p>Items are objects Tamers and Digimon carry, equip, and use. Some are found as loot. Others are bought, and buying means dealing with one of two currencies.</p>\n<h2><strong>Currency</strong></h2>\n<p>DigiDollars (DD) are the currency of the Digital World, what Digimon and Digital World merchants actually deal in. Real World Money is the ordinary money a Tamer already has access to back home, however they'd normally come by it. Some items only ever turn up for sale on one side of that divide and are priced in whatever currency that world uses. Others are available in both places and carry a separate price tag for each, since DD and Real World Money aren't pegged to any fixed exchange rate between them.</p>\n<h2><strong>Item Types</strong></h2>\n<p>The Digivice sits apart from everything else in this chapter as the primary item every Tamer needs, and the other five types below round out the rest of a Tamer's gear.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Rules</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Digivice</td><td>The item every Tamer needs. Its only job is letting a Tamer's partner digivolve, but it has addon slots that can be filled with different Digivice addons drawn from different seasons. One equipped at a time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Worn by the Tamer, goggles, a helmet, a jacket, whatever the item actually is. Provides a permanent boost for as long as the Tamer has it on. One equipped at a time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accessory</td><td>Something a Tamer holds dear, whether it came from something that happened during the adventure or was given to them before it even started. One equipped at a time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gadget</td><td>Equipped rather than consumed. Carries a set number of uses that refresh at each rest, and spending one can hand over a new action or boost a skill check. One equipped at a time; can only be swapped when the Tamer takes a rest.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Supply</td><td>Used up the moment it's used. No equip limit; a Tamer can carry as many as they like.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Food</td><td>What a Tamer or their Digimon eats. Most food provides a boost to something lasting until their next rest. Only one bonus at a time.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A Tamer can only have one of each equippable type active at once: one Digivice, one Equipment, one Accessory, and one Gadget. Food and Supply items are never equipped in the first place, so this limit doesn't touch them.</p>\n<h2><strong>Digivice</strong></h2>\n<p>Every Tamer starts with a Digivice. It is the key item to your bond with your partner Digimon, and it is required to help them digivolve.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Digivice</th><th>Crest</th><th>Cost</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Courage Digivice</td><td>Courage</td><td>0 DD / 0 REM</td><td>Using this Digivice's action, make a skill check with any Courage skill, adding +2 to the roll.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Friendship Digivice</td><td>Friendship</td><td>0 DD / 0 REM</td><td>Using this Digivice's action, make a skill check with any Friendship skill, adding +2 to the roll.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Love Digivice</td><td>Love</td><td>0 DD / 0 REM</td><td>Using this Digivice's action, make a skill check with any Love skill, adding +2 to the roll.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Knowledge Digivice</td><td>Knowledge</td><td>0 DD / 0 REM</td><td>Using this Digivice's action, make a skill check with any Knowledge skill, adding +2 to the roll.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sincerity Digivice</td><td>Sincerity</td><td>0 DD / 0 REM</td><td>Using this Digivice's action, make a skill check with any Sincerity skill, adding +2 to the roll.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reliability Digivice</td><td>Reliability</td><td>0 DD / 0 REM</td><td>Using this Digivice's action, make a skill check with any Reliability skill, adding +2 to the roll.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Equipment</strong></h2>\n<p>Equipment is something a Tamer can swap out whenever they rest, and whatever piece they're wearing adds its bonus for as long as they keep it on. The first five pieces below are all clothing.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Equipment</th><th>Cost (REM)</th><th>Cost (DD)</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cold Weather Gear</td><td>120</td><td>60</td><td>When taking damage from a Wind attack, treat it as if you were Resistant to it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Warm Weather Gear</td><td>120</td><td>60</td><td>When taking damage from a Fire attack, treat it as if you were Resistant to it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Poncho</td><td>120</td><td>60</td><td>When taking damage from a Water attack, treat it as if you were Resistant to it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hiking Gear</td><td>160</td><td>80</td><td>Once between rests, when not in an encounter, make a Field Ops check against DN 10. If you pass, restore 2 Hope.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vest</td><td>200</td><td>100</td><td>Once per encounter, you may subtract 10 damage from a hit. This can be declared after the damage is rolled.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Accessory</strong></h2>\n<p>Accessories are the most personal item type in this chapter, things a Tamer holds onto because of what they mean, not because a shop stocked them. That doesn't stop them from carrying a real mechanical benefit alongside the sentiment. Almost all of them come from the Human World in the first place.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Accessory</th><th>Cost (REM)</th><th>Cost (DD)</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Old Photograph</td><td>60</td><td>0</td><td>Once between rests, using your Basic Action, look at it to steady yourself. Restore 1d8 Hope.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lucky Charm</td><td>80</td><td>0</td><td>Once between rests, reroll one roll you just made and take the new result.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Friendship Bracelet</td><td>60</td><td>0</td><td>Permanent +1 to any Friendship skill check.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Worn Pocket Watch</td><td>60</td><td>0</td><td>Permanent +1 to any Reliability skill check.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cellphone</td><td>70</td><td>0</td><td>Once between rests, look something up. Gain +2 on your next skill check tied to what you found.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Gadget</strong></h2>\n<p>Gadgets carry a set number of uses instead of a single permanent effect, and every use spent comes back once the Tamer rests.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Gadget</th><th>Uses (Refresh at Rest)</th><th>Cost (REM)</th><th>Cost (DD)</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Glasses</td><td>3</td><td>60</td><td>120</td><td>Spend one use to add 1d4 to a Radar check.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flashlight</td><td>4</td><td>45</td><td>90</td><td>Spend one use in a dark environment to create a source of light, letting you see as though the space were lit normally.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Makeup Kit</td><td>3</td><td>60</td><td>120</td><td>Spend one use to add 1d4 to a Rally check.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Small Toy</td><td>3</td><td>60</td><td>120</td><td>Spend one use to add 1d4 to a Tame check.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Supply</strong></h2>\n<p>Supply items are used up the moment they're used, no equip slot, no limit on how many a Tamer can carry at once. Using one normally costs a Basic Action, unless the item itself says otherwise.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Supply</th><th>Cost (REM)</th><th>Cost (DD)</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Bandages</td><td>20</td><td>20</td><td>Restore 4 HP to one target. Can be used on a Tamer or a Digimon.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>First Aid Kit</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>Restore 10 HP to one target and remove one status effect. Can only be used outside of combat.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Antidote</td><td>40</td><td>40</td><td>Remove all Poison from one target immediately.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rubber Ball</td><td>25</td><td>25</td><td>Use before making your Basic Attack this turn. Treat the attack as though it had the [RANGE 4] tag.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nicknacks</td><td>25</td><td>25</td><td>Use to immediately add one extra use to an equipped Gadget.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy Drink</td><td>30</td><td>30</td><td>Restore 2 Hope.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Food</strong></h2>\n<p>Food is what a Tamer and their Digimon actually eat, the kind of thing that can satisfy the feeding requirement from Chapter 13. Every price below covers feeding both the Tamer and their Digimon together; buying enough for just one of the pair costs half the listed price.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th>Food</th><th>Cost (REM)</th><th>Cost (DD)</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Digi-Ration</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>A candy bar or power bar sold in both the Human World and the Digital World. Counts as a meal for both Tamer and Digimon. Prevents hunger.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Instant Noodles</td><td>25</td><td>50</td><td>Once, as a Free Action, restore 1d6 HP.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cheeseburger</td><td>25</td><td>50</td><td>Once, as a Free Action, Add 1d4 damage to your next attack.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Onigiri</td><td>25</td><td>50</td><td>Once, as a Free Action, restore 1d6 Hope.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1500,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb016appendix0016","name":"Chapter 16: Appendix & Quick Reference","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>Chapter 16: Appendix & Quick Reference</strong></h1>\n<h2><strong>Combat At a Glance</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Rule</strong></th><th><strong>Summary</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Turn order</td><td>Digimon = Friendship. Tamers = Friendship x2. Ties broken by higher raw Friendship. Separate turns for Tamers and Digimon.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Movement</td><td>Digimon move Friendship spaces. Tamers move Friendship x2 spaces.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adjacent</td><td>Any of the 8 squares surrounding a character's space (includes diagonals).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Action commitment</td><td>Begin an action, finish it. Movement cannot be split around other actions. Move, Basic, and Digivolving Actions can be taken in any order.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Holding your action</td><td>At the start of your turn, pick someone who hasn't gone yet and act right after them instead. Once per round. Only ever moves you later, never back.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Digivolving Action</td><td>Tamer's turn only, once per round. Requires the Tamer to have their Digivice with them.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sprint</td><td>Basic Action. Move a second time up to your full movement.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Digivice (Move Action)</td><td>Instead of moving, use one of your Digivice's own actions.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dedigivolve (Move Action)</td><td>Instead of moving, drop 1 or 2 stages. Hope cost adjusts before being spent. Once per round each for Tamer and Digimon.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hope cost timing</td><td>Start of TAMER'S TURN, not start of round. 0 Hope = revert to Default Stage.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Push Through</td><td>Basic Action, Tamer only, once per encounter. Skill check DN 5 using a skill linked to your Crest. Success restores Hope equal to the roll.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hit roll</td><td>1d20 + attacker's Courage vs defender's Reliability + 10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Natural 1</td><td>Auto miss, no exceptions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Natural 20</td><td>Auto hit + double final damage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Damage</td><td>PR dice + attacker's Knowledge - defender's Love. Min 1 before multipliers.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Multipliers</td><td>Attribute first, then element. Max x3.0. Min x0.25. Always min 1 final damage.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apply damage</td><td>Subtract from the target's current Health. 0 or below = defeated, out of actions for the rest of the encounter.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ending the fight</td><td>Over once one side alone still has anyone able to act, or one side concedes outright.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Active moves</td><td>Basic Attack always available, plus a Signature Move tied to your current stage and 3 Selected Moves chosen at long rest. The Move Pool only grows.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Call Out</td><td>Free Action; partner gets +1 to next attack roll, lasting until the start of your next turn.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Status on digivolution</td><td>ALL status effects (including Poison and Sleep) are cleared on any digivolution, dedigivolution, or sidedigivolution.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Status Effects At a Glance</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Status</strong></th><th><strong>How to Wake / Clear</strong></th><th></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Burn X,Y</td><td>Lose 1 stack at start of turn.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Freeze</td><td>1d20 + Love vs DN 14 at end of turn, or take damage</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Paralyze X</td><td>Lose 1 stack at start of turn. Natural 20 clears all.</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blind</td><td>Lose 1 stack at start of turn (applies as 2 stacks).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Confuse</td><td>1d20 + Friendship vs DN 14 at start of turn</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Poison X</td><td>Lose 1 stack at start of turn.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Firewall check DN 13 at start of turn, or take damage</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fragment X</td><td>Lose 1 stack at start of turn.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>HP Formulas</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Character</strong></th><th><strong>Formula</strong></th><th><strong>Rank 1 Example</strong></th><th><strong>Rank 5 Example</strong></th><th><strong>Rank 10 Example</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Digimon</td><td>20 + (total Sincerity x 4)</td><td>Sincerity 4 = 36 HP</td><td>Sincerity 12 = 68 HP</td><td>Sincerity 20 = 100 HP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tamer</td><td>12 + (Sincerity rank x 4)</td><td>Rank 1 = 16 HP</td><td>Rank 5 = 32 HP</td><td>Rank 10 = 52 HP</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Hope Pool Quick Reference</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Total EXP Earned</strong></th><th><strong>Hope Pool</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>1,500</td><td>20</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2,000</td><td>25</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3,000</td><td>35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4,000</td><td>40</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5,000</td><td>45</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7,500</td><td>55</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10,000</td><td>65</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15,000</td><td>80</td></tr>\n<tr><td>20,000</td><td>100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>30,000</td><td>125</td></tr>\n<tr><td>40,000</td><td>145</td></tr>\n<tr><td>50,000</td><td>165</td></tr>\n<tr><td>60,000</td><td>185</td></tr>\n<tr><td>70,000 and up</td><td>200</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Digivolution At a Glance</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Rule</strong></th><th><strong>Summary</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Hope pool</td><td>See Hope Pool table above; total EXP earned determines pool size</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stage limit</td><td>Max 2 stages above Default Stage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Digivolution path</td><td>Must follow a valid species path. Unlocked forms on different lines are not accessible from an incompatible current form.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rookie extra forms</td><td>First Rookie form free. Each additional Rookie form costs 50 EXP.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Forced digivolution</td><td>Roll d100 above Threshold = (1 - Hope Spent / Full Cost) x Stage Danger to stay clean. At or under the threshold, the Digimon Dark Digivolves instead.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0 HP at Default Stage</td><td>Default Stage drops by 1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overkill (>50% max HP)</td><td>Default Stage drops an additional 1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Daily rest</td><td>Default Stage +1 up to Max Default Stage</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Resting At a Glance</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Rule</strong></th><th><strong>Summary</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rest cycle</td><td>10 hours resting for every 14 hours adventuring, as a baseline</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What resting restores</td><td>Full HP and full Hope for both Tamer and Digimon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interrupted rest</td><td>Still restores HP and Hope; the party must finish the rest itself at the next opportunity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Feeding</td><td>2 food portions per rest, one for the Tamer and one for their Digimon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Missed rests or meals</td><td>Hope Pool halved at 1, quartered at 2, reduced to one eighth at 3 (see Chapter 13)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>CREDITS</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Tamer's Bond, Core Rulebook</strong></h2>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Creator & Lead Designer</strong> punisher355.mo Created the system and was the primary writer on every section of this book. Also a playtester.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Writing & Digimon Statistics</strong> Lewis Helped write the book, filled out the majority of the Digimon species stat blocks used in the system, and built and maintains the Foundry VTT JSON files. Also a playtester.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Writing</strong> H0lderOfHope Helped write the book. Also a playtester.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Playtesters</strong> punisher355.mo Lewis H0lderOfHope Dark Dragon Leonizard Thank you for your time, your feedback, and your willingness to break things so they could be built better.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Tamer's Bond is a fan-made TTRPG. Digimon is the property of Bandai Namco Entertainment.</p>\n<p>This is an unofficial fan work and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Bandai Namco.</p>\n<p>Playtest Draft v1.0</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1600,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb100dnadigi00100","name":"DNA Digivolution","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h2><strong>DNA Digivolution</strong></h2>\n<p>DNA Digivolution is a special form of Digivolution in which two partner Digimon combine into a single, more powerful Digimon. Unlike normal Digivolution, DNA Digivolution is a temporary transformation that follows its own rules.</p>\n<h3><strong>DNA Digivolution Requirements</strong></h3>\n<p>To DNA Digivolve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two willing Tamers must have their partner Digimon active on the battlefield.</li>\n<li>Both Digimon must meet the requirements of the DNA form.</li>\n<li>At least one of the two Tamers must have the DNA form unlocked.</li>\n<li>Both Digimon must be at the stage required by the DNA form.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The exact stage required to DNA Digivolve depends on the DNA form being used.</p>\n<h3><strong>Initiating DNA Digivolution</strong></h3>\n<p>DNA Digivolution is an exception to the normal Digivolution rules.</p>\n<p>The DNA transformation <strong>does not count as one of the two Digivolutions a Digimon can normally make during an encounter</strong>, and it does not count against the normal limit of being two stages above Default Stage.</p>\n<p>When the requirements are met, both partner Digimon combine and are replaced by their DNA form.</p>\n<p>The DNA form remains active for <strong>6 turns</strong>.</p>\n<p>DNA Digivolution costs <strong>no Hope</strong> to initiate and requires <strong>no Hope to maintain</strong>.</p>\n<h3><strong>DNA Crest Stats</strong></h3>\n<p>The DNA form uses its own <strong>Species Base</strong> for each Crest Stat. The <strong>Tamer Rank</strong> and <strong>Digimon Invested</strong> layers come from the two partners.</p>\n<p>For each Crest Stat:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the higher <strong>Tamer Rank</strong> of the two Tamers.</li>\n<li>Use the higher <strong>Digimon Invested</strong> value of the two partner Digimon.</li>\n<li>Add the DNA form's <strong>Species Base</strong>.</li>\n<li>Apply any <strong>Conditional</strong> modifiers normally.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The two partners' full Crest Stat totals are <strong>not</strong> added together, and the DNA form does not use either partner's Species Base.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> One Tamer has Courage 4 and their Digimon has 3 ranks of Digimon Invested Courage. The other Tamer has Courage 2 and their Digimon has 5 ranks of Digimon Invested Courage. The DNA form has a Species Base Courage of 6.</p>\n<p>The DNA form's Courage is:</p>\n<p><strong>Species Base 6 + higher Tamer Rank 4 + higher Digimon Invested 5 = 15 Courage.</strong></p>\n<h3><strong>DNA HP</strong></h3>\n<p>The DNA form has its own HP and Max HP.</p>\n<p>When DNA Digivolution ends, the DNA form's remaining HP is divided between the two returning Digimon as evenly as possible. Neither Digimon can return with more HP than their own Max HP.</p>\n<p>For example, if the DNA form has 19 HP remaining when the fusion ends, one partner returns with 10 HP and the other returns with 9 HP.</p>\n<h3><strong>DNA Move Pool</strong></h3>\n<p>A DNA Digimon can draw on the techniques of both partners. It has its own <strong>Signature Move</strong>, along with the <strong>Selected Moves of both partner Digimon</strong>.</p>\n<p>When DNA Digivolution begins, the DNA Digimon gains access to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its own DNA Signature Move.</li>\n<li>The 3 Selected Moves of the first partner.</li>\n<li>The 3 Selected Moves of the second partner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This gives the DNA Digimon up to <strong>6 Selected Moves</strong> in addition to its Signature Move.</p>\n<p>All Selected Moves still follow the normal stage requirements from Chapter 11. A DNA Digimon can only use a move if its current Stage is equal to or higher than the Stage at which that move was learned.</p>\n<p>When DNA Digivolution ends, each partner returns with their own Move Pool and Selected Moves unchanged.</p>\n<p>If the DNA form has a Signature Move that the partners have not previously unlocked, that Signature Move is added to <strong>both partners' Move Pools</strong> when the DNA Digivolution ends.</p>\n<h3><strong>Ending DNA Digivolution</strong></h3>\n<p>DNA Digivolution automatically ends after <strong>6 turns</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Tamers may also end the DNA Digivolution early as a Free Action.</p>\n<p>If the DNA form reaches <strong>0 HP</strong>, the DNA Digivolution ends immediately.</p>\n<p>When DNA Digivolution ends, both partner Digimon return at the <strong>Current Stage they had before DNA Digivolution began</strong>.</p>\n<p>The DNA form's status effects are cleared when the transformation ends.</p>\n<p>If the DNA form reaches 0 HP, the normal <strong>0 HP: The Cascade</strong> rules apply to the returning Digimon.</p>\n<h3><strong>Special Digivolution Exception</strong></h3>\n<p>DNA Digivolution does not follow the normal two-stage Digivolution limit.</p>\n<p>A DNA form may therefore reach a Stage that would normally be impossible to reach through the Digimon's two available Digivolutions alone.</p>\n<p>The two partner Digimon must still meet the DNA form's listed requirements before the transformation can occur.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1700,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb200armordigi0200","name":"A New Destiny: Armor Digivolution","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>About This Section</strong></h1>\n<p>This section contains expanded rules introduced by the A New Destiny module. These mechanics are not part of the core rulebook; they only apply when the GM has enabled this module for their campaign.</p>\n<h2><strong>Armor Digivolution</strong></h2>\n<p>Armor Digivolution is an ancient, pseudo-evolutionary process that predates the modern digivolution chain. Rather than climbing through stages by spending Hope, a Digimon draws power directly from a Digi-Egg, a relic tied to that egg's element. The result is not a true evolution. It is a separate kind of power entirely, borrowed rather than earned.</p>\n<h3><strong>Requirements</strong></h3>\n<p>The Tamer must have the matching Digi-Egg item in their possession, along with the D-3 Digivice Addon in order to use it to Armor Digivolve. The Digimon must be a species that can reach that specific Armor form. The Armor form does not need to be one of the Digimon's own unlocked Digivolution forms; a compatible species can use it regardless of which forms are currently unlocked.</p>\n<h3><strong>How It Works</strong></h3>\n<p>There is no Hope cost to initiate Armor Digivolution and none to maintain it, since the Digi-Egg powers the form instead. The Armor form lasts for 6 turns from the moment it is activated, after which it expires automatically and the Digimon reverts to their previous stage. Armor Digivolving does not count towards the 2 stage limit.</p>\n<h3><strong>Digivolving From an Armor Form</strong></h3>\n<p>A Digimon in an Armor form can continue digivolving normally by spending Hope. When the Digimon digivolves past the Armor form the Armor form ends immediately.</p>\n<h3><strong>Expiring Naturally</strong></h3>\n<p>When the 6 turn limit is reached the Armor form expires. The Digimon reverts to whichever stage they were at before Armor Digivolution. Hope costs resume normally on the Tamer's next turn.</p>\n<h3><strong>The Digi-Egg Item</strong></h3>\n<p>Each egg is tied to one specific Armor form and is never consumed when used. It can be used once per encounter.</p>\n<h3><strong>Unlocking Armor Forms</strong></h3>\n<p>Finding a Digi-Egg automatically unlocks the corresponding Armor form for a compatible Digimon. No EXP is required. No additional purchase is needed. The egg is the unlock.</p>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1800,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb300biomerge0300","name":"A New Destiny: Biomerge & Card Slash","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h2><strong>Biomerge Digivolution</strong></h2>\n<p>Biomerge Digivolution is the merging of a Tamer's body and consciousness with their Digimon partner. The result isn't a Tamer riding inside a giant monster. It is one being made of both of them, with a single will and a power that neither could reach alone.</p>\n<p>Biomerge lets a Tamer jump straight to Mega without spending Hope, ignoring the two-stages-above-Default limit.</p>\n<h3><strong>Requirements</strong></h3>\n<p>To Biomerge, the Digimon must have its Mega form unlocked (20,000+ Digimon EXP). The Tamer must have their Digivice with them, as with any other Digivolving Action, and must have the Biomerge Accessory equipped.</p>\n<p>Any Tamer who meets these requirements can Biomerge. Biomerge can only be used once per rest period.</p>\n<h3><strong>Initiating Biomerge: The Corruption Check</strong></h3>\n<p>Biomerge is a Digivolving Action. It uses the same resource as a normal Digivolve and costs no Hope. Before the merge resolves, the Tamer must make a Corruption Check.</p>\n<p><strong>The Corruption Check:</strong> Choose one of the following skills: Firewall, Core Drive, Reinforce, or Ironclad. You can choose a different skill each time you Biomerge. Roll against DN 12.</p>\n<p><strong>On a success, Clean Biomerge:</strong> The Tamer and Digimon merge into a single form at Mega stage. The merge lasts 6 turns.</p>\n<p><strong>On a failure, Corrupted Biomerge:</strong> The form that emerges is corrupted. The Tamer is still inside, off the field under the same rules as a Clean Biomerge, but the form itself follows the standard corruption rules from Chapter 8 of the Core Rulebook.</p>\n<p>The Tamer cannot voluntarily end the Biomerge while the form is corrupted, and it does not run on a timer. It remains until the encounter ends or the form reaches 0 HP.</p>\n<h3><strong>While Merged</strong></h3>\n<p>The Tamer is removed from the battlefield. They cannot be targeted, use items, or act independently.</p>\n<p>The Biomerge form acts on the Tamer's original turn in initiative order. The Digimon's normal turn slot becomes inactive, so the merged form does not act twice.</p>\n<p>The merged form uses the Digimon's full Mega stats normally. No Hope is spent to initiate or maintain the Biomerge.</p>\n<p>A Clean Biomerge lasts for 6 turns unless it is ended early or the encounter ends first. A Corrupted Biomerge has no timer and lasts until the encounter ends or the form reaches 0 HP.</p>\n<p>The Tamer's purchased class abilities remain available through the merged form and can be used on its turn as normal.</p>\n<p>The merged form is locked at Mega stage. Once Biomerge is initiated, no further digivolution of any kind, upward or downward, is possible for the rest of the encounter.</p>\n<h3><strong>Ending Biomerge</strong></h3>\n<p>Biomerge ends when one of the following occurs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A Clean Biomerge's 6-turn duration expires.</li>\n<li>The Tamer spends a Digivolving Action on their turn to revert early. This can only be done with a Clean Biomerge.</li>\n<li>The merged form is reduced to 0 HP.</li>\n<li>The encounter ends.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>If Biomerge ends for any reason other than the merged form reaching 0 HP:</strong> The merged form's remaining HP is split between the Tamer and Digimon however the player chooses. The split does not have to be even, but neither character can receive more HP than their individual maximum HP.</p>\n<p>The Digimon reverts to Default Stage, and the Tamer returns to the battlefield in the space the merged form occupied.</p>\n<p><strong>If the merged form is reduced to 0 HP:</strong> Both the Tamer and Digimon are removed from the encounter. They are treated as Defeated according to Chapter 9 and are out of actions for the rest of the encounter.</p>\n<h3><strong>Biomerge Quick Reference</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Rule</strong></th><th><strong>Clean Biomerge</strong></th><th><strong>Corrupted Biomerge</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Action to initiate</td><td>Digivolving Action</td><td>Digivolving Action</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Corruption check</td><td>DN 12, success</td><td>DN 12, failure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hope cost</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Duration</td><td>6 turns, or ended early</td><td>Until 0 HP or encounter ends</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequency</td><td>Once per rest period</td><td>Once per rest period</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Requires Biomerge Accessory equipped</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tamer on field?</td><td>No, merged and untargetable</td><td>No, still inside and untargetable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Further digivolution?</td><td>No, locked at Mega</td><td>No, locked at Mega</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can end voluntarily?</td><td>Yes, with a Digivolving Action</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>On non-defeat end</td><td>HP split between Tamer and Digimon (player's choice); Digimon reverts to Default Stage</td><td>Same, if it ends because the encounter ends rather than reaching 0 HP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>On 0 HP</td><td>Both removed from encounter (Defeated)</td><td>Both removed from encounter (Defeated)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2><strong>Card Slash System</strong></h2>\n<p>Tamers do not just watch their Digimon fight. They can intervene directly. Modify Cards are physical cards embedded with Digital World data that, when slashed through a Digivice, alter the parameters of their partner in real time.</p>\n<p>Any Tamer can use a card as their Basic Action as long as they have the D-Power Digivice Add-On.</p>\n<h3><strong>Acquiring Cards</strong></h3>\n<p>Cards can be purchased, found, traded, or awarded by the GM as loot.</p>\n<p>Tamers can carry any number of cards since they are small and light, but each card is single-use. Once a card is slashed, it is spent and cannot be used again.</p>\n<p>Some rare cards cannot be purchased. The GM determines how these cards are obtained.</p>\n<h3><strong>Using a Card: The Slash</strong></h3>\n<p>Slashing a card costs the Tamer's Basic Action on their turn, and only one card can be slashed per turn.</p>\n<p>The Tamer must be holding the card and have their D-Power Digivice Add-On equipped. The card's effect applies immediately.</p>\n<p>Cards cannot be slashed on another character's turn unless a class ability or card effect specifically allows it.</p>\n<h3><strong>Card Rarity</strong></h3>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Rarity</strong></th><th><strong>Availability</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Common</td><td>Purchased from card shops</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uncommon</td><td>Limited stock, Digital World vendors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rare</td><td>Quest rewards, loot, or trade only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secret Rare</td><td>GM determined only</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":1900,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb400spirittam400","name":"Spirit Tamer","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1>Spirit Tamer</h1>\n<p><em>An optional rule for players who want to become the Digimon.</em></p>\n<p>Normally a Tamer fights alongside a partner Digimon. The Spirit Tamer rule replaces that partner entirely, the Tamer becomes the Digimon themselves. This is a self-contained opt-in system. Other players at the table are completely unaffected. A GM can enable Spirit Tamer for one player without changing anything about how the rest of the game works.</p>\n<h2>The Rules</h2>\n<p>A Spirit Tamer has no partner Digimon. Instead, the Tamer is the Digimon. They have a single turn in combat, there is no separate Tamer turn and no separate Tamer action economy.</p>\n<p>A Spirit Tamer builds one Move Pool, exactly like a partner Digimon: a Signature Move per stage reached, the Basic Attack, and three Selected Moves refreshed at every long rest. They digivolve, maintain Hope, and follow all standard digivolution rules unless stated otherwise below.</p>\n<h3>Digivolving</h3>\n<p>When a Spirit Tamer digivolves, they themselves become the Digimon. While in that form, all normal Digimon rules apply to them exactly as they would to a partner Digimon at that stage, including HP, stats, moves, the Cascade, and Dark Digivolution.</p>\n<h3>Forms</h3>\n<p>A Spirit Tamer has exactly one Fresh form, one In-Training form, and one Rookie form. They may never gain additional forms at these stages.</p>\n<p>Champion and higher forms are unlocked using the Digimon EXP thresholds from Chapter 8's Digivolution Form Unlocks, the same rules as a standard partner Digimon.</p>\n<h3>EXP</h3>\n<p>A Spirit Tamer's EXP pool is still divided between Tamer and Digimon as normal, even though it's one physical form. The Tamer-half EXP still buys Crest Stats, Skills, and Classes; the Digimon-half EXP still buys Digimon Invested stat ranks and Digivolution Form Unlocks. Both pools track the same total EXP earned, spent completely separately, the same as any Tamer-and-partner pair.</p>\n<h3>Default Stage</h3>\n<p>A Spirit Tamer's Default Stage is always Rookie. It can never be changed, raised, or affected by any rule, ability, item, or story exception. Daily Rest Recovery does not apply to it. Their Max Default Stage is always Rookie as well, and there is no way to adjust that either.</p>\n<h3>Shared Move Pool</h3>\n<p>The Tamer half and Digimon half of a Spirit Tamer share a single Move Pool. At Rookie stage, the Tamer half may use any move already in the pool, and the Digimon half may use any move the Tamer half has added to it. Any bonus that would normally buff a Tamer's Basic Attack also buffs a Spirit Tamer's Basic Attack, and any bonus that would normally buff a Digimon's Basic Attack does too, it works in both directions.</p>\n<h2>Spirit Digivolution</h2>\n<p>Spirit Digivolution is an ancient power that predates the modern digivolution chain, channeled through a Spirit, a fragment of one of the ten Legendary Warriors bound into a physical item. Rather than climbing through stages by spending Hope, a Digimon draws power directly from the Spirit itself. The result is not a true evolution. It is a separate kind of power entirely, borrowed rather than earned.</p>\n<h3>Requirements</h3>\n<p>The Tamer must have the matching Spirit (or Spirits, for forms that draw on more than one) in their possession, along with their Digivice, in order to use it to Spirit Digivolve. The Digimon must be a species that can reach that specific Spirit form. The Spirit form does not need to be one of the Digimon's own unlocked Digivolution forms, a compatible species can use it regardless of which forms are currently unlocked.</p>\n<h3>How It Works</h3>\n<p>There is no Hope cost to initiate Spirit Digivolution and none to maintain it, since the Spirit powers the form instead. The Spirit form lasts for 6 turns from the moment it is activated, after which it expires automatically and the Digimon reverts to their previous stage. Spirit Digivolving does not count towards the 2 stage limit.</p>\n<h3>Digivolving From a Spirit Form</h3>\n<p>A Digimon in a Spirit form can continue digivolving normally by spending Hope. When the Digimon digivolves past the Spirit form the Spirit form ends immediately.</p>\n<h3>Expiring Naturally</h3>\n<p>When the 6 turn limit is reached the Spirit form expires. The Digimon reverts to whichever stage they were at before Spirit Digivolution. Hope costs resume normally on the Tamer's next turn.</p>\n<h3>The Spirit Item</h3>\n<p>Each Spirit is tied to one specific Spirit form and is never consumed when used. It can be used once per encounter. Some Spirit forms draw on more than one Spirit at once (see The Spirits below); every required Spirit must be in the Tamer's possession to activate that form, but each individual Spirit still only needs to be used once per encounter to power its part of the fusion.</p>\n<h3>Unlocking Spirit Forms</h3>\n<p>Finding a Spirit automatically unlocks the corresponding Spirit form for a compatible Digimon. No EXP is required. No additional purchase is needed. The Spirit is the unlock.</p>\n<h3>The Spirits</h3>\n<p>The following Digimon require a Spirit to be held as an item in order to Spirit Digivolve.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Digimon</strong></th><th><strong>Required Spirit</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Agnimon</td><td>Human Spirit of Fire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vritramon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Fire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aldamon</td><td>Both Spirits of Fire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kaiser Greymon</td><td>Human Spirits of Fire, Ice, Wind, Earth & Wood</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wolfmon</td><td>Human Spirit of Light</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Garummon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Light</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Beowolfmon</td><td>Both Spirits of Light</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Magna Garurumon</td><td>Beast Spirits of Light, Thunder, Darkness, Water & Steel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chackmon</td><td>Human Spirit of Ice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blizzarmon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Ice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fairimon</td><td>Human Spirit of Wind</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shutumon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Wind</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blitzmon</td><td>Human Spirit of Thunder</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bolgmon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Thunder</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Duskmon</td><td>Corrupted Human Spirit of Darkness</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Velgrmon</td><td>Corrupted Beast Spirit of Darkness</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Löwemon</td><td>Human Spirit of Darkness</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kaiser Leomon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Darkness</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grumblemon</td><td>Human Spirit of Earth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gigasmon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Earth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arbormon</td><td>Human Spirit of Wood</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Petaldramon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Wood</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ranamon</td><td>Human Spirit of Water</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Calmaramon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Water</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mercurymon</td><td>Human Spirit of Steel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sephirothmon</td><td>Beast Spirit of Steel</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>This Rule Is Opt-In</strong> Spirit Tamer and the Spirit item system are two independent modules. A campaign can use Spirit items, Spirit Tamer, both, or neither, they do not interact with each other.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":2000,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}},{"_id":"rb500burstdigi500","name":"Data Squad: Burst Digivolution","type":"text","title":{"show":false,"level":1},"image":{},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<h1><strong>About This Section</strong></h1>\n<p>This section contains expanded rules introduced by the Data Squad module. These mechanics are not part of the core rulebook, they only apply when the GM has enabled this module for their campaign.</p>\n<h2><strong>Burst Digivolution</strong></h2>\n<p>Burst Digivolution isn't something a Tamer activates. Where Armor, Biomerge, and Spirit Digivolution are all things a Tamer reaches for on purpose, spending an item, a merge, or a relic to power a partner up, Burst Digivolution is the Digimon proving something on its own, in the middle of a fight, without anyone choosing to trigger it.</p>\n<p>It costs no Hope to trigger and no Hope to maintain. It requires the Digivice Burst addon and no Digivolving Action. It rides entirely on the back of the Digimon's own combat rolls, and once it starts, it only ever gets stronger the longer the fight keeps going well.</p>\n<h3><strong>Triggering a Burst</strong></h3>\n<p>Whenever a Digimon lands a natural 20 (a crit, per Chapter 10) on an attack roll, roll a d100 against the number needed for its Current Stage:</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>Current Stage</strong></th><th><strong>Roll Needed (d100, at or under)</strong></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rookie</td><td>10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Champion</td><td>20</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ultimate</td><td>35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mega</td><td>60</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Fresh and In-Training Digimon cannot trigger a Burst. Mega II uses the same number as Mega, the same as it does everywhere else in the rules.</p>\n<p>Roll at or under the listed number and the Digimon starts Bursting immediately.</p>\n<h3><strong>While Bursting</strong></h3>\n<p>Once a Digimon is Bursting, every natural 20 it lands afterward adds another stack automatically. No further trigger roll is needed once Bursting has already started, only the very first crit has to earn it.</p>\n<p>Each stack grants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>+1 to Hit Roll</strong></li>\n<li><strong>+1 to Damage Roll</strong></li>\n<li><strong>+1 Damage Reduction</strong> against incoming hits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are flat bonuses applied directly to the combat math, and they stack with no upper limit for as long as Bursting continues.</p>\n<p>Hope costs are completely unaffected. The Digimon still pays its normal Hope Cost Per Turn for whatever stage it's currently holding, exactly as Chapter 12 already requires. Burst Digivolution is layered entirely on top of that, it changes nothing about how digivolving itself works or what it costs.</p>\n<h3><strong>Losing a Burst</strong></h3>\n<p>A Digimon can still digivolve, dedigivolve, or sidedigivolve freely while Bursting, nothing about Burst Digivolution blocks it. Doing so just costs them the Burst.</p>\n<p>Whenever a digivolution of any kind happens while Bursting, whether that's a Tamer choosing to digivolve or dedigivolve, a forced digivolution succeeding, the Cascade dropping the Digimon's stage at 0 HP, or a Dark Digivolution taking hold, Bursting ends immediately and every stack it had built up is lost. There is no partial loss and no way to protect the stacks once a stage change happens.</p>\n<p>Bursting and all of its stacks also end the moment the encounter itself ends, the same as any other Conditional effect.</p>\n<table><tbody>\n<tr><th><strong>This Rule Is Opt-In</strong> Burst Digivolution is a Season 5 module, independent of Armor Digivolution, Biomerge, and Spirit Digivolution. A campaign can run any combination of these, or none at all, they don't interact with each other unless the GM specifically says otherwise.</th></tr>\n<tr><td>---</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},"video":{"controls":true,"volume":0.5},"src":null,"system":{},"sort":2100,"ownership":{"default":-1},"flags":{}}],"folder":null,"sort":0,"ownership":{"default":0},"flags":{},"_stats":{"systemId":"digital-destiny","systemVersion":"1.2.5","coreVersion":"11","createdTime":null,"modifiedTime":null,"lastModifiedBy":null}}
